@namespace Lumeo
@using System.ComponentModel
@using System.Globalization
@using Lumeo.GanttV3
@using Lumeo.Services
@inject Lumeo.Services.IComponentInteropService Interop
@inject Lumeo.Services.IThemeService Theme
@attribute [EditorBrowsable(EditorBrowsableState.Never)]
@implements IDisposable
@implements IGanttStateHost

@* Gantt3 — v3's root component (design spec Phase 2, T2 "read-only markup";
   working name — renamed in Phase 4 once it replaces the v2 Gantt public API).
   Owns a GanttState instance and renders GanttNav + GanttTimeline against it.
   No drag/resize/progress editing and no JS interop here — that is later
   phases; this is a pure, static read model of GanttScale + GanttState.
   The tree pane (T3) is deliberately NOT built here.

   NOTE (accessibility): see GanttBar.razor's NOTE — the Razor compiler always
   generates a component's class as `public`, so despite the task spec asking
   for `internal`, this stays `public` and its new members are recorded in
   PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt; flagged for the reviewer/team lead to confirm.
   [EditorBrowsable(Never)] keeps this working-name API out of consumer
   IntelliSense until the Phase-4 rename (fix-round follow-up). *@
<div class="@RootClass" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    @* shadcn alignment (2026-08-10 plan, G2 "unified card shell"): the demo
       renders toolbar + canvas as ONE .card (1px border, 12px radius,
       overflow-hidden) with the toolbar as an internal .card-toolbar strip
       sharing the same surface — previously this root was a borderless
       `flex flex-col gap-3` with only the scroll pane bordered, so toolbar
       and canvas read as two floating blocks. RootClass below now owns the
       outer border; GanttNav gets its strip look (rounded top corners,
       bottom divider) via its own Class parameter, and the scroll pane keeps
       its own bottom-corner rounding (see that div's own remarks) — EACH
       piece rounds its own corners rather than the shell clipping via
       overflow-hidden. Deliberate: GanttNav's TrailingContent slot hosts
       GanttSettingsMenu's popover and GanttTimeline hosts BarContextMenu,
       both `position: fixed` DOM descendants that are NOT portaled to
       <body> (see ContextMenuContent.razor/Popover) — an overflow-hidden
       ancestor would clip them whenever they open near an edge. Per-piece
       rounding gets the same unified-card look with zero clipping risk. *@
    <GanttNav ViewMode="@_state.ViewMode"
              ViewModeChanged="HandleViewModeChangedAsync"
              PeriodLabel="@PeriodLabel"
              OnToday="GoToTodayAsync"
              OnPrevious="ShiftToPreviousAsync"
              OnNext="ShiftToNextAsync"
              ZoomLevels="@ZoomLevels"
              TrailingContent="@TrailingContent"
              Class="rounded-t-md border-b border-border px-3 py-2" />

    @* Phase 2's "one shared scroll container wrapping both panes" (T3 design
       spec, scroll sync): tree pane + timeline are laid out side by side as
       plain flex siblings INSIDE this single overflow:auto div, so vertical
       scrolling moves both together with no JS scroll-sync needed yet.
       Codex round 3, P2 #1: this div is now ALSO the timeline's horizontal
       scroll owner (see ScrollHost below) — the timeline no longer nests its
       own overflow-x-auto wrapper, so Blazor's Virtualize correctly resolves
       ITS scroll ancestor to be THIS element (real, height-capped) instead of
       that now-removed inner wrapper (content-sized, so every row measured as
       "visible" — see GanttTimeline.ScrollHost's own remarks for the full
       root-cause). GanttTree becomes sticky-left (see its RootClass) to stay
       visually pinned to this pane's left edge as it now scrolls horizontally
       too.

       Bug fix (Codex round 3, cx3 review): GanttTree's sticky-left ONLY has
       room to actually stick if ITS containing block (this .flex row) is
       genuinely as wide as the scrollable content. Two changes were needed,
       not one — the first attempt (GanttTimeline's "grow shrink-0" + its own
       RootStyle min-width, see that component's remarks) was NOT sufficient
       by itself and still reproduced the bug live: a `display:flex` row's
       OUTER box is a normal BLOCK-level box in normal flow, and a block
       box's width comes from its OWN containing block (this scroll pane),
       NOT from shrink-to-fit over its children's min-width/flex-basis — so
       even with GanttTimeline's flex item correctly refusing to shrink below
       TotalWidth, the .flex row's own measured box (what position:sticky's
       containing block bounds actually resolve against) stayed pinned at
       ~viewport width; only the rendered CONTENT visually spilled past it.
       `width:max-content` below is what makes THIS box itself size to its
       children's combined natural width (same "shrink-to-fit" sizing an
       inline-block/table gets by default, opted into here for a block-level
       flex container) — giving GanttTree's sticky positioning genuine room
       across the full scroll range (reproduced live before this second
       change: bbox still at x=-3114 after scrolling to 70% of maxScroll,
       despite the first change already being in place). *@
    <div class="rounded-b-md" style="max-height:@Height;overflow:auto;" @ref="_scrollPaneRef">
        @* Rows is a computed property (see below) — read it into a local ONCE
           per render and hand the same value to both bindings below, rather
           than letting each binding re-invoke the property (which would run
           GanttRowModel.BuildVisibleRows twice per render for no reason). *@
        @{ var rows = Rows; }
        <div class="flex" style="width:max-content;">
            @if (EffectiveShowTreePane)
            {
                <GanttTree Rows="@rows"
                           OnToggleCollapse="HandleToggleCollapseAsync"
                           TreeColumns="@TreeColumns"
                           TreeHeaderMenu="@TreeHeaderMenu"
                           RowTemplate="@RowTemplate"
                           ShowTaskMeta="@ShowTaskMeta"
                           TreePaneWidth="@EffectiveTreePaneWidth"
                           OnResize="HandleTreePaneResizeAsync"
                           Tasks="@_state.Tasks"
                           SelectedIds="@_state.SelectedIds"
                           ShowRowCheckboxes="@ShowRowCheckboxes"
                           OnRowSelectionChange="HandleRowSelectionChangeAsync"
                           AllowRowReorder="@AllowRowReorder"
                           CanDropRow="CanDropRow"
                           OnRowReorder="HandleRowReorderAsync"
                           Readonly="@Readonly" />
            }
            <GanttTimeline Tasks="@_state.Tasks"
                           Rows="@rows"
                           ViewMode="@_state.ViewMode"
                           ViewModeChanged="HandleViewModeChangedAsync"
                           RangeStart="@_state.VisibleRange.Start"
                           RangeEnd="@_state.VisibleRange.End"
                           BarHeight="@BarHeight"
                           ColumnWidth="@ColumnWidth"
                           TodayHighlight="@TodayHighlight"
                           FirstDayOfWeek="@FirstDayOfWeek"
                           OffDays="@OffDays"
                           MarkOffDays="@MarkOffDays"
                           NowIndicator="@NowIndicator"
                           Now="@_browserNow"
                           BarColor="@BarColor"
                           BarTemplate="@BarTemplate"
                           ScrollToTodayRequestId="@_scrollToTodayRequestId"
                           Today="@EffectiveToday"
                           ScrollTargetDate="@_scrollTargetDate"
                           ScrollTargetXOverride="@_scrollTargetXOverride"
                           ScrollHostLeadingOffset="@ScrollHostLeadingOffset"
                           LeadingPaneWidth="@(EffectiveShowTreePane ? EffectiveTreePaneTotalWidth : 0)"
                           ScrollHost="@_scrollPaneRef"
                           Readonly="@Readonly"
                           OnTaskUpdate="HandleTaskUpdateAsync"
                           OnTaskClick="OnTaskClick"
                           CanDrop="CanDrop"
                           AllowCreate="@AllowCreate"
                           ShowSummaryBars="@ShowSummaryBars"
                           RollupMath="RollupMath"
                           SummaryTemplate="SummaryTemplate"
                           ColorByGroup="@ColorByGroup"
                           ShowOffscreenIndicators="@ShowOffscreenIndicators"
                           ShowZoomControl="@ShowZoomControl"
                           ZoomLevels="@ZoomLevels"
                           DragGhostTemplate="@DragGhostTemplate"
                           BarContextMenu="@BarContextMenu"
                           InfiniteScroll="@InfiniteScroll"
                           OnRangeExtensionRequested="HandleRangeExtensionRequestAsync"
                           Class="grow shrink-0" />
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Hoists this chart's <see cref="GanttV3.GanttState"/> store (design spec Phase
    /// 3, T4 — the REUI <c>useGanttState</c> analog). <c>null</c> (the default) keeps
    /// the PRIOR, fully self-contained behavior: an internally-owned instance,
    /// constructed once at mount, invisible to anything outside this component —
    /// byte-identical to every pre-T4 render (see <see cref="_state"/>'s own remarks
    /// for the mount-time resolution and <c>Gantt3RenderTests</c>'s own byte-identical
    /// golden-markup regression). Supplying an instance lets a consumer read/react to
    /// this chart's state externally and drive it imperatively
    /// (<see cref="GanttV3.GanttState.ScrollToTaskAsync"/>/<see
    /// cref="GanttV3.GanttState.ScrollToDateAsync"/>/<see
    /// cref="GanttV3.GanttState.SetViewMode"/>), and lets multiple components share
    /// ONE instance (see <see cref="GanttV3.GanttState"/>'s own attach/detach
    /// remarks for exactly what "sharing" means: every attached-or-not subscriber
    /// re-renders on any change; only ONE at a time is the imperative-API target).
    ///
    /// Read ONCE, at <c>OnInitialized</c> — like <see cref="ViewMode"/>'s own mount
    /// seed, this is an "owned outright for the component's lifetime" parameter, not
    /// a reactively-rebound one (design spec "keep it simple" precedent, same
    /// rationale <see cref="_state"/>'s predecessor field already used). Changing
    /// WHICH instance is bound after mount is deliberately out of T4's scope — a live
    /// swap would need to rebuild every per-instance reconcile-tracking field
    /// (<c>_lastSnapshot</c>/<c>_tasksVersion</c>/<c>_viewModeIntent</c>/etc.) against
    /// the new instance's already-committed values, which is a different, harder
    /// problem T4 does not need to solve to satisfy the plan's own contract ("nav and
    /// view rendered separately against ONE shared state instance must work" — a
    /// stable instance for the component's lifetime, not a reactive one).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public GanttState? State { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The tasks (and milestones) rendered as bars.</summary>
    [Parameter] public IEnumerable<GanttTask>? Tasks { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised whenever the task set changes — either because <see cref="Tasks"/>
    /// genuinely changed, or because the user dragged/resized a bar in the
    /// chart (design spec Phase 2, T1 — v2-parity <c>TasksChanged</c>, same
    /// contract as v2's <c>Gantt.TasksChanged</c>).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<IEnumerable<GanttTask>> TasksChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The active zoom level. Two-way bindable via <see cref="ViewModeChanged"/>. Default <see cref="GanttViewMode.Day"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public GanttViewMode ViewMode { get; set; } = GanttViewMode.Day;

    /// <summary>Raised when the user picks a new zoom level from <see cref="GanttNav"/>'s toolbar.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GanttViewMode> ViewModeChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>CSS max-height of the timeline's scrollable viewport. Default <c>"500px"</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Height { get; set; } = "500px";

    /// <summary>
    /// Highlight today with an accent header label, header dot, and full-column
    /// tint (design spec Phase 3, T2 — master switch, see
    /// <c>GanttTimeline.TodayHighlight</c>'s own remarks). Default true.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool TodayHighlight { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Explicit first-day-of-week override for the Day-mode ISO week-band upper header (design spec Phase 3, T2). Null derives it from <see cref="CultureInfo.CurrentCulture"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public DayOfWeek? FirstDayOfWeek { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The week days treated as "off" for <see cref="MarkOffDays"/> (design spec Phase 3, T2). Null derives a region-appropriate set from <see cref="CultureInfo.CurrentCulture"/> — see <see cref="GanttScale.DefaultOffDays"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>? OffDays { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Tints <see cref="OffDays"/> columns (design spec Phase 3, T2). Default false — no v2 equivalent.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool MarkOffDays { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Renders a precise current-time line in sub-day view modes (design spec Phase 3, T2). Default false.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool NowIndicator { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Override the pixel height of each task bar (default <see cref="GanttScale.DefaultBarHeight"/>).</summary>
    [Parameter] public int? BarHeight { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Override the pixel width of each date column for the current view mode.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int? ColumnWidth { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Per-task bar colour override — same contract as v2's <c>Gantt.BarColor</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<GanttTask, string?>? BarColor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Optional grouping function. When set, tasks are sorted by group label
    /// (stable within a group) before rendering — mirrors v2's GroupBy sort.
    /// Visual group-header rows are a tree-pane (T3) concern, not built here.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<GanttTask, string>? GroupBy { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Replaces a bar's default label content when set.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<GanttTask>? BarTemplate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when a bar is activated via click or keyboard (Enter/Space) — same contract as v2's <c>Gantt.OnTaskClick</c> (Codex round 4, P2 #5).</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GanttTask> OnTaskClick { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Shows the left tree/list pane (design spec Phase 2, T3). Null (the
    /// default) resolves to <see cref="GanttRowModel.DefaultShowTreePane"/>: on
    /// when <see cref="GroupBy"/> is set or any task sets
    /// <see cref="GanttTask.ParentId"/>, off otherwise — a flat task list with
    /// neither gets the same single-pane layout T2 always rendered.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool? ShowTreePane { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Disables drag-to-move/-resize editing in the chart (design spec Phase 2, T1 — v2-parity <c>Readonly</c>). Default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Readonly { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised after a task bar's start/end dates change via drag/resize, with the updated task (design spec Phase 2, T1 — v2-parity <c>OnDateChange</c>, same contract as v2's <c>Gantt.OnDateChange</c>). Also folded into <see cref="TasksChanged"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GanttTask> OnDateChange { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Unified commit-gate event (design spec Phase 2, T1 — the REUI
    /// <c>onEventUpdate</c> analog): raised for EVERY drag/resize edit
    /// alongside <see cref="OnDateChange"/>, carrying a
    /// <see cref="GanttTaskUpdateSource"/> discriminator so a consumer can
    /// branch on which gesture produced the update without a separate
    /// callback per gesture.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GanttTaskUpdate> OnTaskUpdate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised after a task bar's progress changes via the progress handle (design spec Phase 2, T2 — v2-parity <c>OnProgressChange</c>, same contract as v2's <c>Gantt.OnProgressChange</c>). Also folded into <see cref="TasksChanged"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GanttTask> OnProgressChange { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Live scheduling drag-drop validator (design spec Phase 2, T2 — the REUI <c>canDropEvent</c> analog). See <see cref="GanttTimeline.CanDrop"/> for the full contract; passed straight through unchanged.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<GanttTask, GanttScheduleDropContext, bool>? CanDrop { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Opt-in drag-create on empty track background (design spec Phase 2, T3 — a
    /// REUI-parity addition; v2 has no drag-create at all, so this defaults to
    /// <c>false</c>, keeping strict v2 parity until a caller opts in). Passed
    /// straight through to <see cref="GanttTimeline.AllowCreate"/> — see there for
    /// the full contract, including why <see cref="Readonly"/> always wins over it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool AllowCreate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised when a drag-create commits (design spec Phase 2, T3 — REUI parity;
    /// v2 has no equivalent). Fired alongside <see cref="OnTaskUpdate"/>
    /// (Source=<see cref="GanttTaskUpdateSource.Create"/>) — mirrors how
    /// <see cref="OnProgressChange"/>/<see cref="OnDateChange"/> are each raised
    /// alongside <see cref="OnTaskUpdate"/> for their own gesture, per source, in
    /// <see cref="HandleTaskUpdateAsync"/>. Also folded into <see cref="TasksChanged"/>
    /// (the new task is APPENDED, not merged-by-id, for this source).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GanttTaskUpdate> OnTaskCreate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Renders a duration-weighted REUI-style envelope strip on every
    /// hierarchy-parent or flat-group row (design spec Phase 3, T3 — "Summary
    /// rollup bars, duration-weighted"). Default <c>false</c> — see
    /// <see cref="GanttTimeline.ShowSummaryBars"/>'s own remarks for the
    /// reasoning (a deliberate v3-look delta from REUI's own default-on
    /// <c>summaryBars</c>, pinned in the Phase-3 ledger).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowSummaryBars { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Overrides the default duration-weighted rollup math (design spec Phase
    /// 3, T3 — "Overridable rollup math"). See
    /// <see cref="GanttTimeline.RollupMath"/> for the full contract, including
    /// the defensive handling of a throwing or out-of-range override. Passed
    /// straight through, unchanged.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<IReadOnlyList<GanttTask>, GanttRollup>? RollupMath { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Replaces a summary strip's default rendering when set (design spec
    /// Phase 3, T3 — the REUI <c>renderSummary</c> analog). Passed straight
    /// through to <see cref="GanttTimeline.SummaryTemplate"/>, unchanged.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<GanttRollup>? SummaryTemplate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Assigns each task a stable per-group palette colour when
    /// <see cref="BarColor"/> is null (design spec Phase 3, T7). Passed
    /// straight through to <see cref="GanttTimeline.ColorByGroup"/> — see its
    /// own remarks for the full default-off reasoning and the group-colour
    /// stability contract.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ColorByGroup { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Off-screen indicator chips at a row's edge, click-to-scroll (design
    /// spec Phase 3, T7). Passed straight through to
    /// <see cref="GanttTimeline.ShowOffscreenIndicators"/> — see its own
    /// remarks for the default-TRUE reasoning (the one deliberate exception
    /// to this campaign's default-off precedent for new REUI-parity look
    /// additions).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowOffscreenIndicators { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// Floating +/- zoom control in the canvas corner (design spec Phase 3,
    /// T7). Passed straight through to <see cref="GanttTimeline.ShowZoomControl"/>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowZoomControl { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Restricts/orders which zoom levels BOTH <c>GanttNav</c>'s toolbar
    /// switcher and <see cref="ShowZoomControl"/>'s floating stepper offer
    /// (design spec Phase 3, T7 — fixes a pre-existing gap: <c>GanttNav.ZoomLevels</c>
    /// already existed but was never reachable through <c>Gantt3</c>'s own
    /// surface before this task added a caller for it). Null (the default)
    /// shows the full default Day/Week/Month/Year set on both surfaces — see
    /// <see cref="GanttV3.GanttZoomLevelModel"/> for the exact resolution
    /// contract, including the Quarter-must-be-explicit rule.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<GanttViewMode>? ZoomLevels { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Extra tree-pane columns rendered after the pinned name column (design spec
    /// Phase 3, T5 — REUI "Multi-column task tree"). Null/empty (the default)
    /// renders exactly the single name column T2/T3 already did — byte-identical
    /// unless a caller opts in. See <see cref="GanttV3.GanttTreeColumn"/> for the
    /// per-column contract.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<GanttTreeColumn>? TreeColumns { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Slot rendered in the tree pane's header row, after the column titles
    /// (design spec Phase 3, T5 — REUI "Columns menu in header slot"). Null (the
    /// default) renders nothing extra there.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? TreeHeaderMenu { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Replaces a row's default label-cell content when set (design spec Phase 3,
    /// T5). The indent spacer and expander/toggle button are ALWAYS rendered by
    /// <c>GanttTree</c> regardless — see its own remarks for why that chrome must
    /// survive a custom template or the tree becomes unusable. Only applies to
    /// TASK rows (<c>GanttVisibleRow.Task</c> non-null); a flat-grouping
    /// <c>GanttRowKind.GroupHeader</c> row has no task to feed it and always falls
    /// back to its own label.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<GanttTask>? RowTemplate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Renders a second "date-range · N%" line under a tree row's name
    /// (2026-08-10 shadcn alignment plan, G6). Default <c>false</c> — see
    /// <see cref="Lumeo.GanttTree.ShowTaskMeta"/>'s own remarks (this
    /// parameter is a straight pass-through). Ignored when <see
    /// cref="RowTemplate"/> is set.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowTaskMeta { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Controlled width (pixels) of the tree pane's pinned name column (design
    /// spec Phase 3, T5 — the resizable tree/timeline splitter). Null (the
    /// default) is UNCONTROLLED: the chart tracks its own width internally,
    /// starting at <see cref="GanttV3.GanttScale.DefaultTreePaneWidth"/> and
    /// persisting whatever the user last dragged it to for the rest of the
    /// component's lifetime (session-persistent, not just per-render). Every
    /// value — controlled or internally tracked — is clamped to
    /// [<see cref="GanttV3.GanttScale.MinTreePaneWidth"/>, <see
    /// cref="GanttV3.GanttScale.MaxTreePaneWidth"/>].
    ///
    /// Controlled-ness is decided by THIS parameter's own nullability — NOT by
    /// whether <see cref="TreePaneWidthChanged"/> has a delegate (a bug found
    /// during T5's own disable-check pass: a caller may wire the callback
    /// purely to OBSERVE resizes while leaving this null, which must still
    /// behave as fully uncontrolled/session-persistent, not silently freeze).
    /// See <see cref="TreePaneWidthChanged"/>'s own remarks for the
    /// controlled-veto contract (same "the drag must not fight the parameter"
    /// discipline as every other controlled/uncontrolled pair in this
    /// component).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public double? TreePaneWidth { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised exactly once per completed splitter gesture (a pointer-drag release,
    /// or a keyboard nudge — never continuously while dragging; see
    /// <c>GanttTree</c>'s own remarks for why the LIVE drag visual is JS-only and
    /// never round-trips through Blazor mid-gesture) with the new width, already
    /// clamped. Fires whenever a delegate is present, whether or not
    /// <see cref="TreePaneWidth"/> itself is set — notification and control are
    /// separate concerns (mirrors <c>OnProgressChange</c>/<c>OnDateChange</c>
    /// firing independent of <c>TasksChanged</c>). Control specifically is
    /// decided by <see cref="TreePaneWidth"/>'s own nullability: when it is
    /// non-null (CONTROLLED) and the consumer does not update its own bound
    /// value in response to this callback, that is a veto — the NEXT render
    /// reverts the pane to whatever <see cref="TreePaneWidth"/> still holds,
    /// the same veto contract <c>TasksChanged</c>/<c>ViewModeChanged</c>
    /// already have. The drag never "fights" the parameter, because nothing
    /// round-trips through Blazor until the gesture is already over.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<double> TreePaneWidthChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Renders a checkbox column in the tree pane (design spec Phase 3, T6 — REUI "Leaf row selection (checkboxes)"). Default false. Passed straight through to <see cref="GanttTree.ShowRowCheckboxes"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowRowCheckboxes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Controlled set of selected LEAF task ids (design spec Phase 3, T6 —
    /// two-way <c>SelectedIds</c>). <c>null</c> (the default) is UNCONTROLLED:
    /// the chart tracks its own selection internally via <see
    /// cref="GanttState.SelectedIds"/>, starting empty. Same controlled-ness
    /// discipline as <see cref="TreePaneWidth"/> (decided by THIS parameter's
    /// own nullability, not by whether <see cref="SelectedIdsChanged"/> has a
    /// delegate — a caller may wire the callback purely to OBSERVE selection
    /// while leaving this null). Only LEAF task ids are ever members — see
    /// <see cref="GanttV3.GanttSelectionModel"/>'s own remarks for exactly
    /// which rows qualify and why a hierarchy-parent's own id never appears
    /// here even when every one of its descendants is selected.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public ISet<string>? SelectedIds { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised whenever the selection changes (a checkbox click — leaf toggle
    /// or parent/group select/deselect-descendants), with the FULL resulting
    /// set. Fires whenever a delegate is present, whether or not <see
    /// cref="SelectedIds"/> itself is set — notification and control are
    /// separate concerns (mirrors <see cref="TreePaneWidthChanged"/>'s own
    /// remarks). Control specifically is decided by <see cref="SelectedIds"/>'s
    /// own nullability: when non-null (CONTROLLED) and the consumer does not
    /// update its own bound value in response, that is a veto — the NEXT
    /// render reverts to whatever <see cref="SelectedIds"/> still holds.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<ISet<string>> SelectedIdsChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Enables drag-to-reorder tree rows (design spec Phase 3, T6 — REUI "Row
    /// reorder (drag tree rows)"). Default false. Passed straight through to
    /// <see cref="GanttTree.AllowRowReorder"/> — see its own remarks for the
    /// within-bucket-only scope (cross-parent/cross-group moves are <see
    /// cref="GanttTask.ParentId"/> edits, out of scope, never offered by the
    /// drag UI). <see cref="Readonly"/> blocks it unconditionally.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool AllowRowReorder { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Live validation predicate for a candidate row-reorder drop position
    /// (design spec Phase 3, T6 — the REUI <c>canDropEvent</c> analog applied
    /// to row reorder; see <see cref="GanttV3.GanttScheduleDropContext"/>'s own
    /// remarks for why SCHEDULING drag validation — <see cref="CanDrop"/> —
    /// is a separate, differently-shaped parameter/type from this one).
    /// Passed straight through to <see cref="GanttTree.CanDropRow"/>,
    /// unchanged. Null (default) permits every within-bucket position.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<GanttTask, GanttDropContext, bool>? CanDropRow { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised once, after a completed row-reorder drop, with the resolved
    /// <see cref="GanttRowReorder"/> payload (design spec Phase 3, T6). This
    /// component ALSO applies the equivalent list reorder to its own task set
    /// and pushes it through the SAME <see cref="TasksChanged"/>/controlled-
    /// veto pipeline every other edit (move/resize/progress/create) already
    /// uses — see <see cref="HandleRowReorderAsync"/>'s own remarks for why
    /// the component mutates rather than merely reporting.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GanttRowReorder> OnRowReorder { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Extra content rendered in <see cref="GanttNav"/>'s toolbar, after the zoom
    /// switcher (design spec Phase 3, T8 — the <c>GanttSettingsMenu</c> companion's
    /// documented slot; v2 parity with <c>Gantt.TrailingContent</c>). Passed straight
    /// through to <see cref="GanttNav.TrailingContent"/>, unchanged.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? TrailingContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Overrides the drag ghost's rendered content for every bar (design spec Phase
    /// 3, T8). Passed straight through to <see cref="GanttTimeline.DragGhostTemplate"/>
    /// — see its own remarks (and <see cref="GanttBar.DragGhostTemplate"/>'s) for the
    /// full coordinate-space contract.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<GanttTask>? DragGhostTemplate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Renders a Lumeo <c>ContextMenu</c> at the pointer when a bar is right-clicked
    /// (design spec Phase 3, T8). Passed straight through to
    /// <see cref="GanttTimeline.BarContextMenu"/> — see its own remarks for the full
    /// contract, including why this is deliberately independent of <see cref="Readonly"/>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<GanttTask>? BarContextMenu { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Nearing either horizontal edge of the currently-rendered range extends
    /// it by another page, preserving scroll position, instead of dead-ending
    /// (design spec Phase 3, T9 — "horizontal infinite scroll ... replaces
    /// v2's fixed pad-before/after"). Passed straight through to
    /// <see cref="GanttTimeline.InfiniteScroll"/> — see its own remarks.
    ///
    /// Default <c>true</c> (per the design spec's own literal wording) —
    /// DELIBERATE, decided the same way <see cref="ShowOffscreenIndicators"/>'s
    /// own default-true was: what does an EXISTING (pre-T9) v3 consumer
    /// actually notice? Nothing, for any chart that never scrolls far enough
    /// to reach the range <c>ComputeInitialRange</c>/<c>ApplyPadding</c> already
    /// computed — those two methods are COMPLETELY unchanged by this
    /// parameter, so the chart's INITIAL render, and every render before a
    /// user scrolls near either padded edge, is byte-identical regardless of
    /// this default. The only observable delta is what happens ONCE that edge
    /// is reached: pre-T9 (and <c>false</c>), the timeline simply stops —
    /// native <c>scrollWidth</c> caps there, exactly v2's own fixed-padding
    /// behavior; default-<c>true</c>, it keeps going. That is a strictly
    /// ADDITIVE capability (you can now scroll further than before), never a
    /// visual "look changed on upgrade with no code change" surprise the way
    /// e.g. <see cref="NowIndicator"/>/<see cref="MarkOffDays"/>' own opt-in
    /// defaults exist to avoid (both of those touch EVERY row/column of an
    /// already-fully-visible chart unconditionally the moment they're on;
    /// infinite scroll only ever engages in direct response to the viewer's
    /// own continued scrolling, past a boundary nothing before T9 let them
    /// reach anyway). <c>false</c> restores v2 parity exactly (byte-matching
    /// fixed padding — see the Phase-3 ledger entry for the parity spec that
    /// proves it against both routes).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool InfiniteScroll { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Additional CSS classes merged onto the root element.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Captures unmatched attributes and applies them to the root element.</summary>
    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)] public Dictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    // Design spec Phase 3, T4 — resolved ONCE in OnInitialized, to either the
    // caller-supplied State parameter (hoisted) or a freshly-constructed,
    // internally-owned instance (State null — the byte-identical default; see
    // State's own remarks for why this stays a mount-time resolution, not a
    // reactive one). Everything below that reads/writes `_state` is completely
    // unaware of which case it is in — the ENTIRE hoisting surface is this field's
    // own source (owned vs. supplied) plus the _hoisted-gated attach/subscribe
    // block in OnInitialized/Dispose below.
    private GanttState _state = null!;

    // True exactly when State was non-null at OnInitialized — gates every NEW T4
    // code path (Attach/Detach, the Changed subscription) so State == null takes
    // NONE of them: not merely "no observable difference", the code is literally
    // unreached, which is what makes the default case provably byte-identical
    // rather than "byte-identical by construction of a shared branch".
    private bool _hoisted;

    // Codex round 3, P2 #1 — the shared outer pane's own element reference,
    // handed down to GanttTimeline as ScrollHost so it (and its Virtualize)
    // resolve their scroll ancestor to THIS real, height-capped element
    // instead of a now-removed inner wrapper. See the markup's own remarks.
    private ElementReference _scrollPaneRef;

    // G2 (2026-08-10 shadcn alignment): the shell now owns ONE border wrapping
    // GanttNav + the scroll pane, which are plain block-stacked children (no
    // flex/gap needed for that — normal-flow div stacking). No `overflow`
    // here (see the markup's own remarks: GanttNav's TrailingContent and
    // GanttTimeline's BarContextMenu render position:fixed popovers that
    // would be clipped by an overflow-hidden ancestor) — GanttNav rounds its
    // own top corners and the scroll pane rounds its own bottom corners
    // instead. Uses the full-opacity `border-border` (the S1 token direction)
    // rather than `/60` — this is a new, local surface, not a rewrite of an
    // existing library-wide class string, so it does not touch any shared
    // token or `src/Lumeo/UI` file.
    private string RootClass => Cx.Merge("rounded-md border border-border", Class);

    // The single, shared row list GanttTree/GanttTimeline/GanttArrowLayer all
    // render against (design spec Phase 2, T3 — see GanttRowModel's remarks).
    // Recomputed fresh from _state each render; cheap (a linear pass over the
    // task list) and always in lockstep with _state.Tasks/_state.Collapsed.
    private IReadOnlyList<GanttVisibleRow> Rows => GanttRowModel.BuildVisibleRows(_state.Tasks, _state.Collapsed);

    // Bug fix (Codex round 10 review, P2 #3): DefaultShowTreePane used to
    // check the RAW, unfiltered _state.Tasks for a ParentId — an invalid-
    // duration task (End<Start, non-milestone; see GanttRowModel's own
    // FilterValidDurationTasks) carrying the ONLY ParentId in the list showed
    // the tree pane for what is, in the actually-rendered Rows, a flat chart
    // (that task never reaches Rows at all). Uses the SAME shared filtered
    // set BuildVisibleRows itself filters against, so this default can never
    // disagree with what actually ends up on screen.
    private bool EffectiveShowTreePane =>
        ShowTreePane ?? GanttRowModel.DefaultShowTreePane(GanttRowModel.FilterValidDurationTasks(_state.Tasks), GroupBy is not null);

    // Bug fix (Codex round 5, P1 #4): the ambient text direction, resolved the
    // same way every other RTL-aware Lumeo component does (Carousel/Tabs/
    // Splitter/etc.'s own EffectiveDirection) — a DirectionProvider ancestor
    // wins via the cascaded "LumeoDirection" value; otherwise the global
    // ThemeService's own current direction applies.
    [CascadingParameter(Name = "LumeoDirection")] private LayoutDirection? ScopedDirection { get; set; }
    private LayoutDirection EffectiveDirection => ScopedDirection ?? Theme.CurrentDirection;

    /// <summary>
    /// Pixels of the shared scroll pane's own content that precede the
    /// timeline's origin, forwarded to <c>GanttTimeline.ScrollHostLeadingOffset</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Bug fix (Codex round 5, P1 #4): under RTL, the outer <c>.flex</c> row
    /// wrapping GanttTree + GanttTimeline reverses its children's VISUAL
    /// order (flex's default <c>row-reverse</c>-under-<c>dir="rtl"</c> — the
    /// same mechanism <c>GanttTree.RootClass</c>'s <c>start-0</c> fix relies
    /// on, confirmed live by <c>GanttV3StickyLeftTests</c>' RTL sticky-right
    /// spec) even though DOM order stays [Tree, Timeline] unchanged — so
    /// under RTL, GanttTimeline's own box is laid out FIRST physically (at
    /// the scrollable content's own physical-left origin), with GanttTree
    /// occupying the TRAILING (physically rightmost) chunk instead. The
    /// round-4 fix added the tree's width (then <see cref="GanttScale.DefaultTreePaneWidth"/>,
    /// a fixed constant — design spec Phase 3, T5 made it resizable, see
    /// <see cref="EffectiveTreePaneTotalWidth"/>) unconditionally whenever a
    /// tree pane is shown, which is only correct for LTR (where Tree genuinely
    /// precedes Timeline physically) — under RTL, Timeline's own origin
    /// already sits at the content's physical 0, so adding the tree's width
    /// overshot the target by exactly that amount, landing the centered
    /// viewport a whole tree-width off from where the marker actually needed
    /// to be.
    /// </remarks>
    private double ScrollHostLeadingOffset => GanttViewportGeometry.LeadingOffset(EffectiveShowTreePane, EffectiveDirection, EffectiveTreePaneTotalWidth);

    // ── Tree pane splitter (design spec Phase 3, T5) ────────────────────────
    //
    // Uncontrolled backing store — session-persistent (design spec: "persists
    // during session"). Only ever read/written when TreePaneWidth (the
    // PARAMETER) is null; a controlled caller's own field is the persistence
    // store in that case, matching every other controlled/uncontrolled pair in
    // this component (Tasks/TasksChanged, ViewMode/ViewModeChanged).
    private double _internalTreePaneWidth = GanttScale.DefaultTreePaneWidth;

    // The name column's own resolved width, clamped regardless of source
    // (controlled parameter OR internally-tracked default) — a controlled
    // caller supplying an out-of-range value directly is defended against the
    // same way a hostile/careless JS wire value is (see HandleTreePaneResizeAsync).
    private double EffectiveTreePaneWidth =>
        Math.Clamp(TreePaneWidth ?? _internalTreePaneWidth, GanttScale.MinTreePaneWidth, GanttScale.MaxTreePaneWidth);

    // Extra GanttTreeColumns are fixed-width and NOT individually resizable in
    // T5's scope (see GanttTreeColumn's own remarks) — this is a plain sum, not
    // a clamped/controlled value.
    private double TreeColumnsWidth => TreeColumns is { Count: > 0 } ? TreeColumns.Sum(c => c.Width) : 0;

    // The FULL physical width of the combined tree pane (name column + any
    // extra columns + the reserved TreeHeaderMenu slot) — what actually
    // precedes the timeline's own origin inside the shared scroll container.
    // Feeds ScrollHostLeadingOffset/BuildSnapshot; gated on EffectiveShowTreePane
    // is unnecessary here since LeadingOffset's OWN showTreePane parameter
    // already zeroes the offset when the pane is hidden — this stays a pure
    // "what would the pane's width be if shown". Must stay in lockstep with
    // GanttTree.TotalWidth's own identical formula (Codex review, P2 #8 —
    // GanttScale.TreeHeaderMenuWidth's own remarks): if this component thinks
    // the pane is narrower than what GanttTree ACTUALLY renders, LeadingPaneWidth/
    // ScrollHostLeadingOffset under-report the tree's real on-screen footprint.
    // Must stay in lockstep with GanttTree.TotalWidth's own identical formula
    // (Codex review, P2 #8 — GanttScale.TreeHeaderMenuWidth's own remarks):
    // if this component thinks the pane is narrower than what GanttTree
    // ACTUALLY renders, LeadingPaneWidth/ScrollHostLeadingOffset under-report
    // the tree's real on-screen footprint.
    private double EffectiveTreePaneTotalWidth =>
        EffectiveTreePaneWidth + TreeColumnsWidth + (TreeHeaderMenu is not null ? GanttScale.TreeHeaderMenuWidth : 0);

    // The ONE place a completed splitter gesture (pointer-drag release or
    // keyboard nudge — see GanttTree.OnResize's own remarks) lands. Deliberately
    // NOT routed through ReconcileAsync: a resize never moves any DATE (no
    // range/scroll-target decision to make — see GanttViewportReconciler.Decide's
    // own "geometryChanged only moves the scroll TARGET, never the range" rule),
    // it only changes how many pixels of tree pane precede the timeline's
    // origin. Keeping _lastSnapshot's own TreePaneWidth in sync here (rather
    // than waiting for the next unrelated reconcile to notice the drift via
    // GanttViewportReconciler.Decide's geometryChanged belt-and-braces branch)
    // is what keeps a LATER live-scroll-center capture (e.g. a view-mode switch
    // moments after a resize) decoding against the geometry actually on screen
    // — see GanttViewportSnapshot.TreePaneWidth's own remarks.
    private Task HandleTreePaneResizeAsync(double width)
    {
        // Defense in depth (design spec Phase 3, T5 "min/max clamps"): the JS
        // side already clamps continuously during the live drag (see
        // gantt-v3.js's registerSplitterDrag), so a value reaching here is
        // ordinarily already in range — but this is the JSInvokable boundary's
        // own trust edge (a hostile/buggy caller could invoke
        // GanttTree.CommitSplitterWidth directly), so it is re-clamped here
        // too rather than trusted blindly.
        var clamped = Math.Clamp(width, GanttScale.MinTreePaneWidth, GanttScale.MaxTreePaneWidth);
        if (Math.Abs(clamped - EffectiveTreePaneWidth) < 0.5) return Task.CompletedTask; // no-op guard, same idempotence discipline as GanttState's mutators

        // Bug found during T5's own disable-check pass: this MUST gate on the
        // exact same signal EffectiveTreePaneWidth's own `??` fallback reads
        // (TreePaneWidth's nullability), not on TreePaneWidthChanged.HasDelegate
        // — those two are NOT the same question. A caller can wire
        // TreePaneWidthChanged purely to OBSERVE resizes without ever supplying
        // TreePaneWidth itself (staying null, i.e. genuinely uncontrolled) — an
        // earlier version gated this write on HasDelegate alone, which for that
        // caller silently froze the pane at whatever width it mounted with
        // FOREVER (the write was skipped because a delegate existed, yet nothing
        // else advances _internalTreePaneWidth either, since EffectiveTreePaneWidth
        // still falls through to it whenever TreePaneWidth is null). Gating on
        // TreePaneWidth's own nullability instead keeps write and read
        // perfectly symmetric: this only skips the internal write when a REAL
        // controlling value is present to defer to.
        if (TreePaneWidth is null) _internalTreePaneWidth = clamped;

        // Mirrors EffectiveTreePaneTotalWidth's own formula (Codex review, P2
        // #8) — can't just read that property here: `clamped` is the NEW
        // width, not yet reflected in EffectiveTreePaneWidth when TreePaneWidth
        // is controlled (its own re-render from the caller's TreePaneWidthChanged
        // handler hasn't landed yet).
        var newTotal = clamped + TreeColumnsWidth + (TreeHeaderMenu is not null ? GanttScale.TreeHeaderMenuWidth : 0);
        _lastSnapshot = _lastSnapshot with { TreePaneWidth = newTotal };

        StateHasChanged();
        // Notifying is a SEPARATE concern from ownership — any listener (bound
        // or not) is told the resolved, clamped value every time, mirroring how
        // OnProgressChange/OnDateChange fire regardless of TasksChanged.
        return TreePaneWidthChanged.HasDelegate ? TreePaneWidthChanged.InvokeAsync(clamped) : Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // The effective pixel-per-column width for a given mode — a caller's
    // ColumnWidth override when set, else the mode's own default. Parameterized
    // by mode (rather than reading _state.ViewMode) so a reconcile records the
    // width against the mode it belongs to, not whatever mode happens to be
    // committed at the moment (Codex round 14, finding #1).
    //
    // Bug fix (Codex round 17 review, P2): `> 0` (not `??`) — mirrors
    // GanttTimeline.EffectiveColumnWidth's OWN identical fix (see its own
    // remarks for the full v2-parity reasoning: v2 treats a 0/negative
    // override as "unset" via a plain JS truthy check). Kept consistent with
    // that property deliberately — this value feeds the reconcile's own
    // snapshot/capture math, so if it disagreed with what GanttTimeline
    // actually renders, the reconcile would decide "did ColumnWidth change"
    // and decode captured scroll positions against a DIFFERENT width than
    // the one on screen.
    private int EffectiveColumnWidthFor(GanttViewMode mode) => ColumnWidth > 0 ? ColumnWidth!.Value : GanttScale.GetConfig(mode).ColumnWidth;

    // The resolved tree-pane visibility for an ARBITRARY task set — the same
    // rule as EffectiveShowTreePane (used in markup against _state.Tasks), but
    // parameterized so a reconcile can resolve it for the INCOMING, not-yet-
    // committed tasks (Codex round 10 review, P2 #3 — filter first, so the
    // default can never disagree with the actually-rendered rows).
    private bool ResolveShowTreePane(IReadOnlyList<GanttTask> tasks) =>
        ShowTreePane ?? GanttRowModel.DefaultShowTreePane(GanttRowModel.FilterValidDurationTasks(tasks), GroupBy is not null);

    // Builds the value snapshot of every viewport input for a given task set +
    // mode (Codex round 14 consolidation). The renderable-empty flag uses the
    // FILTERED set — the same one that reaches the panes — so a list of only
    // invalid-duration tasks is correctly seen as empty (finding #2); the
    // column width is resolved against THIS mode, not _state's (finding #1).
    private GanttViewportSnapshot BuildSnapshot(int tasksVersion, IReadOnlyList<GanttTask> tasks, GanttViewMode mode) =>
        new(tasksVersion,
            GanttRowModel.FilterValidDurationTasks(tasks).Count == 0,
            mode,
            EffectiveColumnWidthFor(mode),
            ResolveShowTreePane(tasks),
            EffectiveDirection,
            EffectiveTreePaneTotalWidth);

    // The single viewport reconcile (Codex round 14): capture the live scroll
    // center (when the decision needs it) under the OLD geometry BEFORE
    // committing anything, then commit tasks/mode/range synchronously (one
    // coherent frame — finding #4), then emit the one-shot scroll intent. All
    // shared by the three triggers: OnParametersSetAsync (parameter lifecycle),
    // HandleViewModeChangedAsync (the toolbar), and OnThemeChanged (a global
    // ThemeService direction flip with no parent re-render).
    //
    // Bug fix (Codex round 15 review, P1): an overlapping SECOND call into this
    // method — e.g. a theme flip's OnThemeChanged firing while a parameter-
    // driven ReconcileAsync's own capture await above is still outstanding —
    // could previously finish its OWN (faster) capture-and-commit first, only
    // for the FIRST, slower call to resume afterward and commit on top,
    // clobbering the second call's fresher outcome with a stale one decided
    // under now-superseded geometry. Every call claims the next generation via
    // BeginReconcile() before its own capture await and re-checks
    // IsCurrentReconcile(...) the instant that await resolves: whichever call
    // claimed the NEWEST generation is authoritative, regardless of which
    // call's own interop round-trip happens to resolve first — an
    // earlier-started call that resumes later abandons its ENTIRE commit
    // (including _lastSnapshot) rather than risk overwriting a newer one.
    //
    // Bug fix (Codex round 16 review, P1 finding #2): returns whether this
    // call actually committed (true) or was superseded/abandoned (false) — a
    // caller with its OWN post-reconcile side effects (HandleViewModeChangedAsync's
    // ViewModeChanged callback; OnThemeChanged's StateHasChanged) must suppress
    // them too when abandoned, not just the commit itself. The abandon check
    // below already covered the commit AND the scroll-intent emit (both sit
    // after it); this only adds the RETURN VALUE so callers outside this
    // method can extend the same guard to their own side effects.
    private async Task<GanttReconcileOutcome> ReconcileAsync(IReadOnlyList<GanttTask> nextTasks, GanttViewMode nextMode)
    {
        // Bug fix (Codex PR-382 review round 7, P2 "Stop Today replay after
        // component disposal"): the ONE central refusal to start new work on a
        // disposed component. Round 17's disposal guard advances the generation,
        // which invalidates any reconcile that later re-checks a generation it
        // claimed EARLIER — but every path that starts fresh work after an await
        // claims a NEW generation, so that mechanism is structurally blind to it.
        // Round 6 patched one such site (the rejection replay); this is the whole
        // class instead: every commit, every scroll intent and every interop call
        // in this component funnels through this method, so refusing here covers
        // the navigation replays, the theme replay, the rejection replay and any
        // future caller, and the per-site check round 6 added is now redundant
        // (removed). Callers already treat Superseded as "do not run your tail".
        if (_disposed) return GanttReconcileOutcome.Superseded;

        var prev = _lastSnapshot;
        var tasksChanged = _state.WouldChangeTasks(nextTasks);
        var nextTasksVersion = tasksChanged ? _tasksVersion + 1 : _tasksVersion;
        var next = BuildSnapshot(nextTasksVersion, nextTasks, nextMode);

        // Bug fix (Codex round 16 review, P2 finding #4): a nonempty->nonempty
        // task replacement into a DISJOINT date range (no overlap with the
        // range currently in effect) used to fall through Decide's own "no
        // emptiness transition, no mode/geometry change" branch as a plain
        // target=None — the range itself rebuilds around the new tasks, but
        // nothing ever requests a recenter, so the DOM's own untouched scroll
        // position (meaningful only under the OLD range) lands on empty space
        // under the brand-new, unrelated one. Computed here as pure range math
        // (no interop needed, so it's safe to do before the capture phase) and
        // folded into Decide as an extra fact alongside the pre-existing
        // emptiness-transition trigger — computed ONCE and reused by the
        // COMMIT phase below instead of calling ComputeInitialRange twice.
        var prospectiveTaskRange = tasksChanged
            ? ComputeInitialRange(nextMode, nextTasks, EffectiveToday)
            : (GanttDateRange?)null;
        var taskRangeDisjoint = tasksChanged &&
            (prospectiveTaskRange!.Value.End < _state.VisibleRange.Start ||
             prospectiveTaskRange.Value.Start > _state.VisibleRange.End);

        var decision = GanttViewportReconciler.Decide(prev, next, taskRangeDisjoint);

        // Bug fix (Codex round 17 review, P1): an unrelated parent re-render
        // (e.g. a Class-only change, or literally the same values re-pushed)
        // reaches OnParametersSetAsync's own unconditional ReconcileAsync call
        // on EVERY pass — before this fix, THIS call would still run all the
        // way down to BeginReconcile() below even when Decide finds NOTHING
        // changed at all, claiming a generation for no reason and silently
        // aborting a genuinely useful, currently-suspended reconcile (e.g. a
        // toolbar mode switch whose own capture await is still outstanding) —
        // the exact same class of bug round 16's own theme-notification fix
        // closed for the OnThemeChanged path, now closed here for the
        // parameter path. Range==Keep && Target==None is provably "nothing in
        // the diff changed at all" (see GanttViewportReconciler.Decide's own
        // structure: tasksChanged always forces TaskDerived, viewModeChanged
        // always forces SelfCenteredOnCapture/CapturedCenter, so Keep+None can
        // only result when tasksChanged/viewModeChanged/geometryChanged are
        // ALL false) — which also means `next` is field-for-field identical
        // to `prev` already, so there is nothing left to do; abandon before
        // ever claiming a generation.
        if (decision.IsNoOp)
            return GanttReconcileOutcome.NoOp;

        // CAPTURE PHASE — read the pane's live center under the OLD geometry,
        // BEFORE any state mutation, so the intermediate render Blazor Server
        // can interleave at this await still shows a fully OLD, coherent frame
        // (finding #4: the old code committed the new tasks first, exposing a
        // half-reconciled frame — new tasks, un-moved scroll).
        //
        // Bug fix (Codex round 16 review, P2 finding #5): passes prev.Direction
        // explicitly now too (was: leadingOffset/columnWidth only) — see
        // ResolveCurrentCenterDateAsync's own remarks for why the live DOM
        // direction can't be trusted here for a ThemeService-driven flip.
        var generation = BeginReconcile();
        DateTime? capturedCenter = null;
        if (decision.NeedsLiveCenterCapture)
            capturedCenter = await ResolveCurrentCenterDateAsync(prev.LeadingOffset, prev.ColumnWidth, prev.Direction);

        // Bug fix (Codex round 15 review, P1): abandon entirely if a NEWER
        // reconcile has already claimed a later generation while the capture
        // above was in flight — that newer call already committed (or will
        // commit) fresher state derived from a more current snapshot; this
        // call's own decision/capture were computed against an OLD prev/next
        // pair that no longer reflects reality, so nothing below (including
        // the _lastSnapshot tail write) may run.
        if (!IsCurrentReconcile(generation)) return GanttReconcileOutcome.Superseded;

        // COMMIT PHASE — synchronous from here to the end, so the next render is
        // one coherent frame (tasks, mode, and range all move together).
        if (tasksChanged)
        {
            _state.SetTasks(nextTasks);
            _tasksVersion = nextTasksVersion;
        }
        _state.CommitViewMode(nextMode); // no-op when unchanged

        switch (decision.Range)
        {
            case GanttRangeSource.TaskDerived:
                _state.SetVisibleRange(prospectiveTaskRange!.Value);
                break;
            case GanttRangeSource.SelfCenteredOnCapture:
                // A genuine mode switch reshapes the range around the captured
                // center. Align the center to the target mode's own unit
                // boundary for the range's min/max (Codex round 11, P2 #2 — a
                // live-scroll center carries arbitrary minutes in sub-day
                // modes), but keep the RAW center as the recenter target below.
                var aligned = GanttScale.AlignToUnitStart(_state.ViewMode, capturedCenter!.Value);
                _state.SetVisibleRange(ApplyPadding(_state.ViewMode, aligned, aligned));
                break;
            // GanttRangeSource.Keep: VisibleRange untouched (only the target moves).
        }

        // EMIT PHASE — one expiring scroll intent (consumed exactly once by
        // GanttTimeline; see EmitScrollIntent).
        switch (decision.Target)
        {
            case GanttScrollTarget.CapturedCenter:
                EmitScrollIntent(capturedCenter);
                break;
            case GanttScrollTarget.TodayOrMidpoint:
                // An emptiness transition (or, since round 16, a disjoint
                // task-range replacement): Today when it falls inside the new
                // range (always true for the empty-list fallback, which is
                // centered on Today by construction), else the new range's own
                // midpoint (Codex round 9's delta from v2, for far-from-today
                // async-loaded tasks).
                var range = _state.VisibleRange;
                var todayInRange = EffectiveToday >= range.Start && EffectiveToday <= range.End;
                EmitScrollIntent(todayInRange ? null : VisibleCenterDate);
                break;
            // GanttScrollTarget.None: no scroll — the DOM keeps its position.
        }

        _lastSnapshot = next;
        return GanttReconcileOutcome.Committed;
    }

    // Issues a one-shot scroll intent: a target date (null = Today) plus a
    // version bump. GanttTimeline honors the target on the version bump ONLY,
    // and treats it as consumed thereafter — a later ambient re-scroll (a
    // browser-"today" change at midnight, a host swap) targets Today, never a
    // stale preserved center (Codex round 14, finding #3).
    //
    // Design spec Phase 3, T9 — pixelOverride (null for every EXISTING
    // caller, unchanged behavior): see GanttTimeline.ScrollTargetXOverride's
    // own remarks for why ONE caller (HandleRangeExtensionRequestAsync's
    // leading-edge recenter) needs a pixel-exact target the DateTime-based
    // `target` parameter cannot express. Always overwritten (never merged
    // with a stale previous value) so a LATER plain-date intent correctly
    // clears any earlier pixel override.
    private void EmitScrollIntent(DateTime? target, double? pixelOverride = null)
    {
        _scrollTargetDate = target;
        _scrollTargetXOverride = pixelOverride;
        _scrollToTodayRequestId++;
    }

    private Task HandleToggleCollapseAsync(string key)
    {
        _state.ToggleCollapsed(key);
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // Design spec Phase 3, T6 — bubbled from GanttTree.OnRowSelectionChange
    // exactly like HandleToggleCollapseAsync above: Gantt3 is the only place
    // that touches GanttState, so it resolves the tri-state
    // "select/deselect descendants" semantics here via
    // GanttSelectionModel.ResolveLeafIds rather than GanttTree computing it
    // itself (GanttTree stays purely presentational — see its own class
    // remarks).
    //
    // Unconditionally writes to _state (mirrors HandleTaskUpdateAsync's own
    // "commits DIRECTLY to _state" discipline) — a CONTROLLED SelectedIds
    // consumer that doesn't accept the change simply re-supplies its OWN
    // current value on the next render, which OnParametersSetAsync's own
    // SelectedIds push (see its own remarks) reasserts, reverting the local
    // change — the same veto contract every other controlled/uncontrolled
    // pair in this component already has.
    private async Task HandleRowSelectionChangeAsync((string Key, bool Selected) change)
    {
        if (Readonly) return; // defensive server-side guard (design spec Phase 3, T6 "Readonly blocks both") — GanttTree's own Checkbox.Disabled=Readonly already prevents the click client-side; this is the untrusted-boundary backstop every JSInvokable in this campaign already applies to ITS OWN surface, extended here to a plain EventCallback for the same reason

        var targets = GanttSelectionModel.ResolveLeafIds(GanttRowModel.FilterValidDurationTasks(_state.Tasks), change.Key);
        if (targets.Count == 0) return;

        var anyChanged = false;
        foreach (var id in targets)
        {
            if (_state.IsSelected(id) != change.Selected) anyChanged = true;
            _state.SetSelected(id, change.Selected);
        }
        if (!anyChanged) return; // genuine no-op (every target already matched) — no spurious notification, same idempotence discipline as every GanttState mutator

        if (SelectedIdsChanged.HasDelegate) await SelectedIdsChanged.InvokeAsync(new HashSet<string>(_state.SelectedIds, StringComparer.Ordinal));
    }

    // The whole viewport-tracking state (Codex round 14): ONE snapshot of the
    // last reconciled inputs, replacing the six per-parameter fields rounds
    // 11-13 accreted (_lastSeenViewMode/_lastPushedViewMode/_lastSeenDirection/
    // _lastSeenEffectiveShowTreePane/_lastSeenColumnWidth/_rangeInitialized).
    // Every "did X change" question is now one diff against this snapshot.
    private GanttViewportSnapshot _lastSnapshot;
    private bool _initialized;

    // Monotonic task-set version — bumped only when the effective task set
    // actually changes (GanttState.WouldChangeTasks). A diff on the snapshot's
    // copy of this is the exact tasksChanged signal, kept as a plain value so
    // the reconcile decision is a pure snapshot diff.
    private int _tasksVersion;

    // Codex round 15 review, P1 — see ReconcileAsync's own remarks. Every
    // reconcile call claims the next generation (synchronously, before its own
    // capture await can yield to anything else queued on the renderer's sync
    // context) and re-checks it immediately after that await resolves: a
    // mismatch means some OTHER call started later and is now the authoritative
    // one, so this call must abandon its entire commit.
    private long _reconcileGeneration;

    private long BeginReconcile() => ++_reconcileGeneration;

    private bool IsCurrentReconcile(long generation) => generation == _reconcileGeneration;

    // Bug fix (Codex round 18 review, P1): a direct-navigation action
    // (prev/next/Today) mutates VisibleRange OUTSIDE the reconcile pipeline. It
    // must SUPERSEDE any reconcile currently suspended in its live-center capture
    // await — that reconcile claimed an EARLIER generation and, on resume, would
    // otherwise still pass its IsCurrentReconcile check and commit its now-stale
    // mode/range on top of the navigated one (the DOM would jump back to where a
    // mode switch had been reshaping toward, discarding the nav the user just
    // performed).
    //
    // Bug fix (Codex round 18-f2 review, P1 findings #1/#2): superseding must
    // NOT silently drop the superseded reconcile's own pending PARAMETER inputs
    // (a mode and/or task-set change the parent pushed that hadn't committed
    // yet). After navigating (which only moved the range), RE-APPLY the current
    // parameters against the just-navigated state with a FRESH capture — so the
    // pending change still lands (no stale jump), or a no-op if nothing was
    // pending. The re-apply's own reconcile claims a later generation, so the
    // original suspended reconcile still abandons; the navigation stands AND the
    // pending delta lands.
    // Bug fix (Codex PR-382 review, P1, lines ~1028/~1056): returns the
    // re-apply's own outcome so callers (ShiftAsync, GoToTodayAsync) can react
    // to it — specifically, detect a Superseded result and bail out of their
    // OWN subsequent direct-mutation tail instead of unconditionally running it
    // on top of whatever a NEWER action already committed.
    private async Task<GanttReconcileOutcome> SupersedeReconcileForNavigationAsync()
    {
        BeginReconcile(); // supersede any in-flight reconcile (its capture is now stale)
        return await ReapplyCurrentParametersAsync();
    }

    // Re-runs the reconcile after a supersession (a navigation or a theme flip),
    // so the superseded pass's pending inputs still land (Codex round 18-f2).
    //
    // Codex PR-382 review round 3 consolidation: this is the REPLAY path, and it
    // deliberately does not look at the ViewMode PARAMETER at all. A navigation
    // never decides a view mode — it destroys the reconcile that was carrying the
    // current intent, so its whole job here is to re-execute THAT intent. Reading
    // the parameter instead is what produced findings #1, #5, #6 and #7 in
    // sequence: for a CONTROLLED binding the parameter still holds the pre-pick
    // value until the consumer's own (possibly async) ViewModeChanged handler has
    // returned AND the parent has re-rendered, and for an UNCONTROLLED one it
    // holds a value a competing trigger may have deliberately overridden. Both
    // are answered by the intent's own ParamAccountedFor/Phase instead — see
    // GanttViewModeIntent's remarks.
    //
    // Tasks stay parameter-derived, but from the MATERIALIZED snapshot rather
    // than the live Tasks enumerable — a replay is not a parameter pass, so it
    // must not enumerate a possibly single-pass sequence a second time (see
    // _materializedTasks). ReconcileAsync still diffs that snapshot structurally,
    // so a replay with nothing pending is a no-op and a pending replacement the
    // superseded pass materialized but never committed still lands (round 18-f2
    // finding #2).
    private async Task<GanttReconcileOutcome> ReapplyCurrentParametersAsync()
    {
        var intent = _viewModeIntent;
        var outcome = await ReconcileAsync(_materializedTasks, intent.IsPending ? intent.Mode : _state.ViewMode);
        if (outcome == GanttReconcileOutcome.Superseded) return outcome;
        return await SettleViewModeIntentAsync(intent.Id, outcome);
    }

    // ── View-mode ownership: ONE value, ONE claim point, ONE settle point ──
    //
    // Codex PR-382 review round 3 consolidation. Rounds 17-20 spread this across
    // _pendingToolbarMode / _committedViewModeParam / _lastSeenViewModeParam plus
    // a MarkViewModeParamAccountedFor helper trying to keep two of them in step;
    // every one of findings #1-#7 was a different hand-maintained LIFETIME among
    // those fields going wrong at a different call site, and each fix exposed the
    // next. _viewModeIntent answers the whole question in one place — see
    // GanttViewModeIntent's own remarks for the four facts it carries. Three
    // rules, each stated exactly once in this file:
    //
    //  1. CLAIM — a new intent is created in exactly three places (design spec
    //     Phase 3, T4 added the third): the toolbar (HandleViewModeChangedAsync),
    //     a parameter pass whose ViewMode says something genuinely new
    //     (ResolveParameterMode), and an imperative GanttState.SetViewMode call
    //     (IGanttStateHost.SetViewModeAsync) — the latter two both funnel through
    //     ApplyViewModeIntentAsync's own single ClaimViewModeIntent call, tagged
    //     with a different GanttViewModeSource, so this is still ONE call site
    //     for "claim", not three independent ones. Claiming REPLACES the
    //     previous intent outright, which is what makes supersession a single
    //     decision rather than something re-derived per call site.
    //  2. REPLAY — ANYTHING that supersedes an in-flight reconcile must re-execute
    //     the intent it superseded, while that intent IsPending
    //     (ReapplyCurrentParametersAsync). That is a navigation, a theme flip,
    //     and — since Codex PR-382 review round 4, finding "Replay a pending mode
    //     during parameter passes" — a parameter pass that COMMITS without
    //     authoring a mode of its own (a tasks-only or geometry-only update
    //     arriving while a mode reconcile is suspended in its capture). Stated as
    //     a rule: a NON-CLAIMING pass must CARRY a pending intent, never resolve
    //     it away. A replay never authors an intent, and never reads the
    //     parameter for a mode.
    //  3. SETTLE — an intent stops owning the view mode in exactly one place
    //     (SettleViewModeIntentAsync), only once its consumer callback (if it
    //     owes one) has actually come back, and only if it is still the intent
    //     that pass claimed. That exit is also where a CONTROLLED consumer's
    //     verdict is read: if the parent re-delivered its ViewMode while we were
    //     notifying and that value is not the pick, the pick was rejected and
    //     the parameter wins (Codex PR-382 review round 5). Advancing "the
    //     parameter has been accounted for" is that transition's by-product, not
    //     a separate field to remember.
    private GanttViewModeIntent _viewModeIntent;
    private long _viewModeIntentId;

    // Claims a NEW intent, superseding whatever owned the view mode before. The
    // ViewMode parameter value visible right now is recorded as accounted for by
    // construction: for a Parameter source it IS the value being applied, and for
    // a Toolbar source the pick deliberately overrides it, which is precisely
    // what "accounted for" means (round 20 finding B, now structural). This is
    // also the eager advance round 17's finding #1 needed — it happens before the
    // reconcile can yield, so an unrelated re-render re-pushing that same
    // in-flight value diffs to a no-op instead of superseding it.
    //
    // Claiming also SUPERSEDES whatever reconcile was in flight, by advancing the
    // generation here rather than leaving it to the claimer's own ReconcileAsync
    // (Codex PR-382 review round 6, P1 "Supersede older reconciles for no-op
    // parameter reversions"). Leaving it there made supersession depend on the
    // claimer having something to commit: a parent that pushes Day->Month and
    // then quickly reverts to Day claims a second, authoritative intent whose own
    // reconcile is a NoOp — Day is still the last committed snapshot — so it
    // abandoned before ever claiming a generation (round 17's short-circuit), and
    // the still-suspended Month reconcile resumed, passed its now-unchallenged
    // generation check, and committed Month over the parent's final Day. A new
    // intent means the old one is no longer authoritative, which is exactly what
    // the generation encodes, so the two move together: the claim IS the
    // supersession, whether or not the claimer itself has work to do.
    //
    // Bug fix (Codex PR-382 review round 7, P1 "Let accepted mode echoes finish
    // the navigation"): only a CONTESTING claim supersedes. Round 6 made every
    // claim advance the generation, which is right for a claim that replaces what
    // the current owner wanted, but wrong for one that merely RATIFIES it — a
    // controlled parent echoing back the very mode we just told it about arrives
    // as a parameter claim too, and counting that as a competitor made the
    // navigation replaying that intent report itself Superseded and skip its own
    // tail (GoToTodayAsync's recenter, ShiftAsync's re-shift). A claim carrying
    // the SAME mode the current intent already wants changes nothing about what
    // is authoritative, so it invalidates nothing; a claim carrying a different
    // one does. Anything the ratifying pass itself genuinely needs to do (a task
    // or geometry change riding along in the same render) still claims its own
    // generation inside ReconcileAsync, so no real work goes unguarded.
    private GanttViewModeIntent ClaimViewModeIntent(GanttViewMode mode, GanttViewModeSource source)
    {
        if (mode != _viewModeIntent.Mode) BeginReconcile();
        return _viewModeIntent = new GanttViewModeIntent(
            ++_viewModeIntentId, mode, source, GanttViewModePhase.Applying, ViewMode, ViewModeChanged.HasDelegate);
    }

    // The mode a PARAMETER pass reconciles to, plus the id of the intent it
    // claimed (null when it claimed none).
    //
    // The parameter may author an intent only when it says something the current
    // owner has not already folded in. Otherwise this pass has nothing to say
    // about the mode at all and reconciles to the COMMITTED one — a no-op in the
    // mode dimension that leaves an in-flight intent completely untouched. That
    // single rule replaces both of the old controlled/uncontrolled branches: the
    // old CONTROLLED branch treated ViewMode as unconditionally authoritative,
    // which is only true of a value the parent has actually just pushed — the
    // very assumption finding #7 broke, since a controlled parent's ViewMode
    // still reads as the OLD mode for as long as its own ViewModeChanged handler
    // is awaiting. A parent that genuinely wants the old mode back (declining a
    // toolbar pick) pushes it as a change against ParamAccountedFor and still
    // wins, exactly as before.
    private (GanttViewMode Mode, long? ClaimedIntentId) ResolveParameterMode()
    {
        // Becoming CONTROLLED is itself a new assertion, even with an unchanged
        // value (Codex PR-382 review round 6). An uncontrolled chart may
        // legitimately sit on a toolbar mode its parameter never carried — that
        // is the whole point of the uncontrolled contract (round 19 finding #2) —
        // but the moment a consumer opts into control, its parameter is
        // authoritative again and the divergence has to be resolved. The reverse
        // transition deliberately does NOT claim: dropping the delegate hands
        // authority back to the component, it does not make the parameter
        // authoritative.
        var bindingBecameControlled = ViewModeChanged.HasDelegate && !_viewModeIntent.AccountedWhileControlled;

        if (!bindingBecameControlled && ViewMode == _viewModeIntent.ParamAccountedFor)
        {
            // A re-delivery of the value the current owner already accounted
            // for. Mid-pass this is still a no-op — nothing reconciles here, so
            // a pending pick keeps running (round-3 finding 7). But if the owner
            // is NOTIFYING, this pass is the parent SPEAKING while we hold its
            // answer pending, so record it: Notifying's exit condition consults
            // it to tell an accepted pick from a rejected one (Codex PR-382
            // review round 5). See SettleViewModeIntentAsync.
            //
            // Only while the binding is actually CONTROLLED, though (Codex
            // PR-382 review round 7, P2 "Respect removal of the controlled
            // binding while notifying"): a parent that has dropped its
            // ViewModeChanged delegate has no vote on the mode at all, so its
            // stale ViewMode is not a verdict — it is the ordinary uncontrolled
            // re-push that must never revert a toolbar pick (round 19 finding
            // #2). Recording it anyway let a removed binding retroactively
            // reject a pick it no longer had any authority over.
            if (_viewModeIntent.Phase == GanttViewModePhase.Notifying && ViewModeChanged.HasDelegate)
                _viewModeIntent = _viewModeIntent with { ParamRedeliveredWhileNotifying = true };
            return (_state.ViewMode, null);
        }

        var claimed = ClaimViewModeIntent(ViewMode, GanttViewModeSource.Parameter);
        return (claimed.Mode, claimed.Id);
    }

    // The ONE place an intent stops owning the view mode.
    //
    // Called by every trigger that reaches a non-superseded outcome, with the id
    // of the intent THAT pass is responsible for. Everything the old three fields
    // needed hand-maintained sequencing for falls out of the three guards below:
    //
    //  * id mismatch — a NEWER intent (any source) already took ownership; this
    //    pass must not settle, clear or echo anything (findings #1/#6, and the
    //    "don't clobber a newer toolbar request" half of #7).
    //  * already Settled — nothing outstanding; a replay that found nothing
    //    pending must not re-advance anything (finding #2's NoOp case is simply
    //    a settle like any other, not a special branch).
    //  * already Notifying — its own echo owns the settle; a replay landing in
    //    that window re-commits the mode but must not fire the consumer callback
    //    a second time.
    //
    // The Notifying phase itself is finding #7: a CONTROLLED toolbar pick is not
    // settled by committing it, because the consumer's handler may yield for an
    // arbitrarily long time and the parent's ViewMode parameter still holds the
    // old value throughout. The intent stays PENDING across that await, so any
    // navigation landing in the window replays this mode instead of resurrecting
    // the stale parameter — and only once the callback actually returns does
    // ParamAccountedFor advance to the mode we told the parent to hold.
    //
    // The generation re-check after the callback is round 20 finding A, unchanged
    // in substance: a caller with its own post-reconcile tail (GoToTodayAsync's
    // recenter, ShiftAsync's re-shift) must not run it on top of whatever a newer
    // action committed while this callback was yielding.
    private async Task<GanttReconcileOutcome> SettleViewModeIntentAsync(long intentId, GanttReconcileOutcome outcome)
    {
        var intent = _viewModeIntent;
        if (intent.Id != intentId) return outcome;
        if (intent.Phase != GanttViewModePhase.Applying) return outcome;

        // Design spec Phase 3, T4 — Imperative (a GanttState.SetViewMode call)
        // owes the SAME consumer echo Toolbar does: from a controlled parent's
        // point of view, an imperative pick and a user's own toolbar click are
        // indistinguishable ("the component decided a new mode, ratify it or
        // not") — see GanttViewModeSource.Imperative's own remarks.
        var owesConsumerEcho = (intent.Source == GanttViewModeSource.Toolbar || intent.Source == GanttViewModeSource.Imperative)
            && ViewModeChanged.HasDelegate
            && outcome == GanttReconcileOutcome.Committed;

        if (!owesConsumerEcho)
        {
            // Nothing outstanding: the parent is left holding whatever it
            // already holds, and that value is now accounted for — together with
            // the authority it currently has (see AccountedWhileControlled).
            _viewModeIntent = AccountParameter(intent, ViewMode, GanttViewModePhase.Settled);
            return outcome;
        }

        _viewModeIntent = intent with { Phase = GanttViewModePhase.Notifying };
        var generationAtCommit = _reconcileGeneration;
        await ViewModeChanged.InvokeAsync(intent.Mode);

        // Bug fix (Codex PR-382 review round 6, P2 "Stop rejection replay after
        // component disposal"): a consumer callback can outlive the component.
        // Round 17's disposal guard works by advancing the generation, which
        // invalidates anything that CHECKS a generation it claimed earlier — but
        // the rejection replay below claims a FRESH one, so that mechanism cannot
        // see it, and it would go on to mutate state, emit a scroll intent and
        // touch the detached scroll pane through interop. This is the one place
        // that starts genuinely NEW work after an await, so it needs the explicit
        // token rather than the generation.
        // A newer intent (any source) took ownership while the callback ran —
        // including the CONTROLLED-accept case, where the parent adopted the
        // pick and re-rendered, and that pass claimed an intent of its own.
        if (_viewModeIntent.Id != intentId || _viewModeIntent.Phase != GanttViewModePhase.Notifying)
            return _reconcileGeneration == generationAtCommit ? outcome : GanttReconcileOutcome.Superseded;

        // ── Notifying's exit condition (Codex PR-382 review round 5, P1
        //    "Preserve controlled-mode rejections during callback renders") ──
        //
        // The window is over, so the parent's opinion becomes actionable. Two
        // passes look IDENTICAL from inside the component at the moment they
        // arrive — a callback echo from a parent that has not decided yet, and a
        // re-push from a parent that has decided to REJECT — because both carry
        // the pre-pick ViewMode and neither exposes anything else to key off.
        // The consumer's own callback task is no discriminator either: Blazor's
        // ComponentBase.HandleEventAsync calls StateHasChanged BEFORE that task
        // completes, so the render carrying a rejection arrives while the task
        // we are awaiting is still incomplete.
        //
        // They ARE distinguishable in TIME, though, which is why this is decided
        // at settle rather than on arrival: by the moment the callback returns,
        // the parent has had its chance. So the rule is —
        //
        //     if the parent SPOKE while we were notifying and its most recent
        //     word is not the pick, the pick was rejected; if the parent said
        //     NOTHING, the pick stands and we expect the parameter to become it.
        //
        // Deciding on arrival instead would revert mid-window and break round-3
        // finding 7 (a legitimately-late parent must keep its pick alive across
        // an arbitrarily long handler); deciding never — the round-3/4 behavior
        // this fixes — leaves the chart showing a mode the authoritative
        // parameter never accepted, with nothing scheduled to correct it, since
        // ParamAccountedFor would swallow the very re-push that carried the
        // rejection.
        //
        // Residual, accepted deliberately: a parent that re-renders BEFORE its
        // own async handler assigns, and never renders again, reads as a
        // rejection. Blazor itself rules that out for a component receiver —
        // HandleEventAsync calls StateHasChanged again on completion — so the
        // adopted value arrives right after and simply claims. A brief revert in
        // that race is strictly better than a chart permanently out of sync with
        // its own parameter.
        // ...and only while the parameter still HAS a vote. A binding removed
        // during the callback leaves the component uncontrolled, so its last
        // ViewMode is not a verdict to act on (round 7, ":613"); authority is
        // therefore checked when the verdict is READ, not only when it was
        // recorded.
        if (_viewModeIntent.ParamRedeliveredWhileNotifying && ViewModeChanged.HasDelegate && ViewMode != intent.Mode)
        {
            var rejected = ClaimViewModeIntent(ViewMode, GanttViewModeSource.Parameter);
            var rejectOutcome = await ReconcileAsync(_materializedTasks, rejected.Mode);
            if (rejectOutcome != GanttReconcileOutcome.Superseded)
                await SettleViewModeIntentAsync(rejected.Id, rejectOutcome);
            // This pass's own post-actions must not run on top of the reverted
            // state — the parent overruled it, exactly like a supersession.
            return GanttReconcileOutcome.Superseded;
        }

        // The pick stands. What the parent should now be holding depends on
        // whether it is still listening: a consumer we echoed to is expected to
        // hold the picked mode, but if the binding was dropped during the
        // callback there is nobody to have adopted it, and the accounting must
        // fall back to the parameter's live value — otherwise the now-UNCONTROLLED
        // component would read the parent's unchanged ViewMode as a change on the
        // next pass and revert the pick it is entitled to keep (round 7, ":613").
        _viewModeIntent = AccountParameter(
            _viewModeIntent,
            ViewModeChanged.HasDelegate ? intent.Mode : ViewMode,
            GanttViewModePhase.Settled);
        return _reconcileGeneration == generationAtCommit ? outcome : GanttReconcileOutcome.Superseded;
    }

    // The single expression that records "this parameter value, under this
    // authority, is now folded into the owning intent". Kept in one place so the
    // value and the controlled-ness can never be advanced apart — the same
    // reason ParamAccountedFor lives on the intent rather than in a field of its
    // own (see GanttViewModeIntent's remarks).
    private GanttViewModeIntent AccountParameter(GanttViewModeIntent intent, GanttViewMode paramValue, GanttViewModePhase phase) =>
        intent with
        {
            Phase = phase,
            ParamAccountedFor = paramValue,
            AccountedWhileControlled = ViewModeChanged.HasDelegate,
        };

    // Browser-local "today" (Codex round 2, P2 #9) — resolved once via JS on
    // Gantt3's first post-render; DateTime.Today (the SERVER's local date) is
    // the fallback used for every render before that resolves, including
    // prerendering (where the JS call itself never runs at all — see
    // OnAfterRenderAsync below, which only fires post-circuit).
    private DateTime? _browserToday;
    private DateTime EffectiveToday => _browserToday ?? DateTime.Today;

    // Codex round 7 review / cx7b, Important #2 — see the staleness check in
    // OnAfterRenderAsync below. The SERVER's own current day the last time
    // that check ran (NOT the browser's cached day) — deliberately a
    // separate value from _browserToday so the check's trigger condition
    // never depends on how far apart the server's and browser's clocks are.
    private DateTime? _lastCheckedServerDay;

    // Phase 2, T1 — Tasks/TasksChanged controlled/uncontrolled discriminators,
    // ported from v2 Gantt.razor's _lastParentHash/_lastPushedTasksHash (see
    // OnParametersSetAsync's remarks for why these are now needed: a drag
    // commit mutates _state directly, bypassing the Tasks parameter).
    private int _lastParentTasksHash;
    private int _lastPushedTasksHash;

    private static int ComputeTasksHash(IReadOnlyList<GanttTask> tasks)
    {
        var hash = new HashCode();
        foreach (var t in tasks)
        {
            hash.Add(t.Id);
            hash.Add(t.Name);
            hash.Add(t.Start);
            hash.Add(t.End);
            hash.Add(t.Progress);
            hash.Add(t.CustomClass);
            hash.Add(t.IsMilestone);
            hash.Add(t.GroupLabel);
            hash.Add(t.ParentId);
            if (t.Dependencies is not null)
                foreach (var d in t.Dependencies) hash.Add(d);
        }
        return hash.ToHashCode();
    }

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Design spec Phase 3, T4 — resolve `_state` FIRST, before anything below
        // touches it (see State's and _state's own remarks for why this is a
        // one-time, mount-only resolution). State null takes the IDENTICAL branch
        // every pre-T4 render took (`new GanttState()`); _hoisted stays false, so
        // the Attach/Changed-subscription block below never runs at all for that
        // case.
        _hoisted = State is not null;
        _state = State ?? new GanttState();
        if (_hoisted)
        {
            _state.Attach(this);
            _state.Changed += OnSharedStateChanged;
        }

        // The mount intent: born Settled, since OnInitialized commits it below
        // synchronously and there is no consumer to notify about a value the
        // consumer itself supplied.
        _viewModeIntent = new GanttViewModeIntent(
            _viewModeIntentId, ViewMode, GanttViewModeSource.Mount, GanttViewModePhase.Settled,
            ViewMode, ViewModeChanged.HasDelegate);
        _state.CommitViewMode(ViewMode);
        // Merge note (phase-2 x master #382): MaterializeTasks() is still the
        // one legitimate mount-time enumeration of the Tasks parameter (its
        // own contract below — every replay reads _materializedTasks instead
        // of re-enumerating a possibly single-pass sequence), it just also
        // seeds the phase-2 T1 hash baseline (see OnParametersSetAsync's own
        // remarks) so the very first drag edit has something to compare
        // against.
        //
        // Bug fix (Codex P1 "Preserve uncontrolled edits when GroupBy
        // changes"): the seed hash is taken from the RAW parameter, not the
        // Sorted (GroupBy-applied) one — see MaterializeTasks' and
        // OnParametersSetAsync's own remarks. Seeding it from the grouped
        // shape would mismatch every SUBSEQUENT pass's now-raw-based hash by
        // construction whenever GroupBy is set at all, making the very
        // SECOND parameter pass look like a parent change regardless of
        // whether anything actually moved.
        var (rawTasks, materializedTasks) = MaterializeTasks();
        _state.SetTasks(materializedTasks);
        // Design spec Phase 3, T6 — seed _state.SelectedIds from a CONTROLLED
        // initial value, same "push into _state, single source of truth"
        // discipline Tasks/ViewMode already use, but WITHOUT their hash-diff/
        // drag-ordering complexity: a selection edit has no viewport/scroll
        // implication, so a plain "push whenever controlled" push (here, and
        // again every OnParametersSetAsync pass below) is the whole contract —
        // see HandleRowSelectionChangeAsync's own remarks for the write side.
        if (SelectedIds is not null) _state.SetSelectedIds(SelectedIds);
        // Seeds the false->true edge-detector at the MOUNT value (see
        // _lastNowIndicator's own remarks) — a chart mounted with the option
        // already on needs no synthetic "transition" here at all, since
        // OnAfterRenderAsync's firstRender below already resolves _browserNow
        // unconditionally for that case.
        _lastNowIndicator = NowIndicator;
        _lastParentTasksHash = _lastPushedTasksHash = ComputeTasksHash(rawTasks);
        _tasksVersion = 1;
        _state.SetVisibleRange(ComputeInitialRange(_state.ViewMode, _state.Tasks, EffectiveToday));
        // Seed the reconcile baseline from the just-committed mount state; the
        // first OnParametersSetAsync then just flips _initialized and returns
        // (GanttTimeline's OWN firstRender performs the initial center).
        _lastSnapshot = BuildSnapshot(_tasksVersion, _state.Tasks, _state.ViewMode);

        // Bug fix (Codex round 9 review, P2 #7): the direction-flip/tree-
        // pane-offset reconcile in OnParametersSetAsync only ever runs when a
        // PARENT re-renders this component (Blazor's parameter lifecycle) —
        // a DirectionProvider ancestor toggling its Direction PARAMETER goes
        // through exactly that path (the cascading value change forces a
        // re-render), but the global ThemeService's own CurrentDirection
        // changing directly (no DirectionProvider ancestor at all — see
        // EffectiveDirection's own ScopedDirection ?? Theme.CurrentDirection
        // fallback) raises ONLY Theme.OnThemeChanged, with no parent re-
        // render involved at all, so OnParametersSetAsync never runs and the
        // flip went unnoticed. Subscribes here the same way every other
        // theme-aware Lumeo component does (see ThemeToggle.razor's own
        // OnThemeChanged/Dispose pair) and unsubscribes in Dispose below.
        Theme.OnThemeChanged += OnThemeChanged;
    }

    // Bug fix (Codex round 9 review, P2 #7) — see OnInitialized's own
    // remarks. Mirrors ThemeToggle's handler shape (a plain void delegate for
    // the Action-typed event), but this component's own reconcile needs an
    // interop round-trip, so the async work is dispatched via InvokeAsync
    // rather than done inline — InvokeAsync also marshals back onto the
    // renderer's sync context, matching every other OnThemeChanged handler.
    private void OnThemeChanged()
    {
        _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
        {
            // Bug fix (Codex round 16 review, P1 finding #1): ThemeService.OnThemeChanged
            // fires for ANY theme change, not just a direction flip — a pure
            // color-mode toggle (light/dark) leaves EffectiveDirection completely
            // unchanged. ReconcileAsync unconditionally claims a new generation
            // via BeginReconcile() before it even decides whether anything needs
            // to change (round 15's own generation guard) — so a no-op
            // notification like this one would still claim the newest
            // generation, silently aborting a genuinely in-flight, direction-
            // relevant reconcile (e.g. a view-mode switch whose own capture await
            // was still outstanding) with nothing useful committed to replace
            // it. Checking the ACTUAL trigger (direction) before ever entering
            // ReconcileAsync means a no-op notification never claims a
            // generation at all. Compared against _lastSnapshot.Direction (the
            // last COMMITTED direction) rather than some separately-tracked
            // field, for the same reason GanttViewportReconciler.Decide's own
            // diff uses it — an in-flight reconcile hasn't updated it yet, so
            // this still reflects "has direction changed since the last known
            // committed state," consistent with what Decide would also compute.
            if (EffectiveDirection == _lastSnapshot.Direction) return;

            // Reconcile the direction flip. Bug fix (Codex round 18-f2): route
            // through the CURRENT-parameters re-apply rather than _state.Tasks/
            // _state.ViewMode — the theme flip SUPERSEDES any in-flight parameter
            // reconcile (BeginReconcile inside ReconcileAsync), so it must carry
            // that reconcile's pending mode/task delta forward too, not just the
            // direction, or the delta evaporates exactly as it did on the nav
            // path. When nothing is pending this reduces to the old behavior
            // (incomingMode == _state.ViewMode, tasks unchanged) plus the
            // direction change the snapshot diff picks up as a geometry change.
            //
            // Bug fix (Codex round 16 review, P1 finding #2): only re-render if
            // this call actually committed — a superseded reconcile has nothing
            // new to show, and StateHasChanged here would be a redundant,
            // information-free render on top of whatever the superseding
            // reconcile's own caller already triggered.
            var outcome = await ReapplyCurrentParametersAsync();
            if (outcome == GanttReconcileOutcome.Committed) StateHasChanged();
        });
    }

    // Design spec Phase 3, T4 — hoisted-state reactivity. When State is supplied
    // externally (_hoisted), this component is no longer the ONLY mutator of
    // _state: a sibling Gantt3 sharing the same instance, or an imperative
    // GanttState.ScrollToTaskAsync/ScrollToDateAsync/SetViewMode call routed to
    // WHICHEVER instance is currently attached (see IGanttStateHost below), can
    // change it without going through any of THIS component's own event
    // handlers — nothing else here would know to re-render. Subscribed ONLY
    // when _hoisted (see OnInitialized) — an owned _state (State null) never
    // adds this subscription, so that path is not merely "no observable
    // difference", the handler is never invoked at all.
    //
    // Every markup binding in this file already reads `_state.*` directly
    // (`<GanttNav ViewMode="@_state.ViewMode">`, `<GanttTimeline Tasks="@_state.Tasks"
    // ...>`, etc.) rather than through any per-component cached copy, so a bare
    // StateHasChanged() here is SUFFICIENT to bring this instance's rendered
    // output back in sync with the shared store after an external mutation —
    // this component's own private reconcile bookkeeping (_lastSnapshot/
    // _tasksVersion/_viewModeIntent) only governs ITS OWN future scroll/
    // recenter decisions, not whether its current markup is correct.
    //
    // Dispatched via InvokeAsync (discarded, matching OnThemeChanged's own
    // fire-and-forget precedent) — a sibling's own mutation happens on the SAME
    // renderer/circuit's sync context (both components necessarily share one,
    // since they hold a live reference to the same C# object), so this is
    // already on the right thread in every case this campaign's Blazor Server/
    // WASM hosting model produces; InvokeAsync costs nothing extra when already
    // there and is the one safe way to handle it if that assumption is ever
    // violated by a future caller.
    private void OnSharedStateChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        if (!_disposed) StateHasChanged();
    });

    // ── IGanttStateHost — the attach contract's host side (design spec Phase 3, T4) ──
    //
    // EXPLICIT interface implementation: keeps these two members OFF Gantt3's own
    // public surface (already forced public by the Razor compiler — see the class
    // remarks at the top of this file) and reachable ONLY through a GanttState's
    // internal IGanttStateHost reference (i.e. only GanttState itself, via Attach,
    // ever calls them) — never directly on a Gantt3 instance, and never a
    // PublicAPI.Unshipped entry (internal-interface members are not part of the
    // shipped surface even though the class implementing them is public).

    Task IGanttStateHost.ScrollToDateAsync(DateTime date) => InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        if (_disposed) return;
        EmitScrollIntent(date);
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    // Routes through the SAME CLAIM-based ownership pipeline HandleViewModeChangedAsync
    // (the toolbar) uses — see ApplyViewModeIntentAsync's own remarks for why this is
    // what stops an imperative SetViewMode from reopening PR #382's bug class: this is
    // not a new mutation path, it is the existing hardened one, entered from a third
    // call site instead of two.
    Task IGanttStateHost.SetViewModeAsync(GanttViewMode mode) =>
        InvokeAsync(() => ApplyViewModeIntentAsync(mode, GanttViewModeSource.Imperative));

    // Codex PR-382 review round 6, P2 — see SettleViewModeIntentAsync's own
    // remarks. Deliberately NOT a general replacement for round 17's
    // generation-based disposal guard (which still covers every suspended
    // reconcile): this token exists for the one path that starts NEW,
    // generation-claiming work after an await a consumer controls.
    private bool _disposed;

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _disposed = true;
        Theme.OnThemeChanged -= OnThemeChanged;

        // Design spec Phase 3, T4 — the explicit half of the attach/detach
        // contract (see GanttState.Detach's own remarks for the weak-reference
        // safety-net half). Unconditional whenever this instance attached at all
        // (_hoisted), regardless of whether it is STILL the currently-attached
        // one — Detach itself is the no-op-safe check for "was I superseded by a
        // later Attach" (a component disposed after being replaced by a sibling
        // sharing the same State must not clear the NEWER attachment).
        if (_hoisted)
        {
            _state.Changed -= OnSharedStateChanged;
            _state.Detach(this);
        }

        // Bug fix (Codex round 17 review, P2): a reconcile currently suspended
        // awaiting the live-center capture (ResolveCurrentCenterDateAsync)
        // used to have NOTHING invalidate it on disposal — only unsubscribing
        // from the theme event above, which does nothing for a reconcile
        // already in flight via any of the OTHER two triggers (a parameter
        // pass, a toolbar click). Its resumed continuation would still commit
        // to _state, call StateHasChanged, or fire ViewModeChanged on an
        // already-disposed component. Claiming one final generation here
        // reuses the EXISTING abandon mechanism (round 15) rather than a
        // second, independent "_disposed" flag: any suspended reconcile's own
        // IsCurrentReconcile check fails the instant it resumes, so it
        // abandons cleanly — no further state mutation, no interop, no
        // callback — through the exact same path an ordinary superseding
        // reconcile already takes.
        BeginReconcile();
    }

    // Codex round 2, P2 #9 — see EffectiveToday's remarks. Resolved once via
    // JS on firstRender; re-checked on later renders too (Codex round 7
    // review, P2 #3 — see RefreshBrowserTodayAsync's own remarks for why a
    // long-mounted chart needs more than a single resolution).
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            // Bug fix (Codex round 3, P2 #1): _scrollPaneRef (this component's
            // own outer-pane @ref, handed to GanttTimeline as ScrollHost) is
            // already valid by THIS point (a component's own ref to its own
            // element is populated before ITS OnAfterRenderAsync runs) — but
            // GanttTimeline's ScrollHost PARAMETER during the render that
            // produced it was fixed at whatever _scrollPaneRef held when this
            // component's OWN render tree was BUILT, which precedes ref
            // capture for that SAME pass (confirmed empirically: GanttTimeline
            // observes an empty/uncaptured ElementReference on its own
            // firstRender even though _scrollPaneRef is already valid here).
            // A parent needing its own newly-captured ref available to a
            // child on mount is a known Blazor pattern requiring one extra
            // forced render — GanttTimeline treats the ref transitioning from
            // "not yet captured" to "captured" as a scroll-reattempt trigger
            // (see its own OnAfterRenderAsync remarks), so the retry isn't
            // silently dropped once this second render delivers the real value.
            StateHasChanged();

            await RefreshBrowserTodayAsync();
            await RefreshBrowserNowAsync();
            // Seeds the server-day baseline the staleness check below
            // compares against, so the very next render doesn't immediately
            // re-query again just because _lastCheckedServerDay was still
            // null (this firstRender pass already resolved the value).
            _lastCheckedServerDay = DateTime.Today.Date;
            return;
        }

        // Bug fix (Codex round 7 review, P2 #3): the ORIGINAL fix resolved
        // _browserToday exactly ONCE (firstRender) on the stated assumption
        // that "the calendar day essentially never changes mid-session" — not
        // true for a chart left open across local midnight (a dashboard tab
        // kept open overnight, say), which then reported yesterday as "today"
        // for the rest of its lifetime: stale Today marker/highlight column,
        // and a Today-button click that recentered on the wrong day.
        //
        // Bug fix (Codex round 7 review / cx7b, Important #2): the FIRST
        // attempt at this gated the re-query behind "the server's current day
        // is past the CACHED BROWSER day" — which sounds equivalent but isn't:
        // for a user whose browser is WEST of the server (the server's local
        // midnight lands hours before theirs), that condition stays TRUE for
        // the entire gap between the server's midnight and the browser's own
        // — every render in that (potentially many-hour) window re-queried
        // the browser, each one correctly finding nothing had actually
        // changed yet. A full JS round-trip on every render defeats the whole
        // point of gating this at all.
        //
        // Fixed by comparing the server's day against the server's OWN day at
        // the last check (_lastCheckedServerDay), not against the browser's
        // cached value — this can only become true once per SERVER calendar
        // day, however many renders happen in between, regardless of how far
        // apart the server's and browser's clocks are. _lastCheckedServerDay
        // is updated unconditionally on every check (not only when a
        // re-query actually happens), so a render landing just after the
        // server's midnight consumes the one allotted check for that whole
        // day even if the browser itself hasn't rolled over yet (still
        // correctly a no-op inside RefreshBrowserTodayAsync in that case).
        // Trade-off, accepted deliberately: a user WEST of the server whose
        // own midnight falls AFTER the server's may not get re-checked again
        // until the FOLLOWING server midnight, rather than immediately after
        // their own — same "eventually correct, not instantly" contract the
        // original design already had, just now bounded to at most one
        // interop call per server day instead of thrashing every render. The
        // Today button's own unconditional refresh remains the instant-
        // correction path for any user who actually needs it right now.
        var serverToday = DateTime.Today.Date;
        var serverDayAdvanced = _lastCheckedServerDay is null || serverToday > _lastCheckedServerDay.Value;
        _lastCheckedServerDay = serverToday;
        if (serverDayAdvanced)
        {
            await RefreshBrowserTodayAsync();
            await RefreshBrowserNowAsync();
        }
    }

    // Queries the browser's local date via JS and reconciles it against the
    // cached _browserToday (Codex round 2, P2 #9; re-armed for re-use beyond
    // firstRender in round 7 review, P2 #3). Safe to call as often as a
    // caller likes — a no-op re-query (unchanged day) is cheap, and the
    // interop call itself already tolerates prerendering/JSDisconnected via
    // GanttV3GetLocalDateAsync's own null-return contract.
    private async Task RefreshBrowserTodayAsync()
    {
        var iso = await Interop.GanttV3GetLocalDateAsync();
        // Disposed while that round-trip was in flight — the only other place in
        // this component that mutates state after an await without going through
        // ReconcileAsync's own central guard (round 7, ":1416").
        if (_disposed) return;
        if (iso is null) return; // interop unavailable (prerendering; a torn-down circuit) — keep whatever fallback EffectiveToday currently resolves to
        if (!DateTime.TryParseExact(iso, "yyyy-MM-dd", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, DateTimeStyles.None, out var browserToday)) return;

        var changedDay = browserToday.Date != EffectiveToday.Date;
        _browserToday = browserToday.Date;
        if (!changedDay) return; // server and browser already agree on the calendar day — nothing to correct

        // VisibleRange itself is only ever SEEDED from "today" when the task
        // list is EMPTY (ComputeInitialRange's fallback branch) — the common
        // (non-empty tasks) case derives its window from the tasks' own
        // min/max dates, unaffected by which day "today" is, so there is
        // nothing stale to recompute there. The empty-list window WAS seeded
        // from the (possibly wrong) server-Today at OnInitialized time, so it
        // gets one corrective recompute here, now that the browser's actual
        // date is known.
        //
        // Bug fix (Codex round 15 review, P2): this used to check the RAW,
        // unfiltered _state.Tasks.Count — the same class of bug BuildSnapshot's
        // own RenderableEmpty already closed for the reconcile pass (round 14,
        // finding #2), just missed here since this correction runs OUTSIDE
        // ReconcileAsync entirely. A task list containing ONLY invalid-duration
        // tasks (End<Start, non-milestone) is non-empty by raw count but
        // renders as an empty chart (GanttRowModel.FilterValidDurationTasks
        // drops them all before ComputeInitialRange's own min/max branch ever
        // sees them) — ComputeInitialRange would internally already fall
        // through to its today-based branch for such a list, but this OUTER
        // gate, using the wrong (raw) count, never even attempted the
        // recompute, so the stale server-Today-seeded range survived a
        // browser-Today correction it genuinely needed. Uses the SAME filtered
        // set every other emptiness decision in this file uses.
        if (GanttRowModel.FilterValidDurationTasks(_state.Tasks).Count == 0)
            _state.SetVisibleRange(ComputeInitialRange(_state.ViewMode, _state.Tasks, EffectiveToday));

        // Re-renders GanttTimeline's Today parameter too (marker position /
        // scroll-to-today math) with the corrected date.
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // Design spec Phase 3, T2 — browser-resolved "now" backing GanttTimeline's
    // NowIndicator param. Refreshed at the SAME points RefreshBrowserTodayAsync
    // already resolves _browserToday (mount, the once-per-server-day staleness
    // check, and an explicit Today-button click — see each call site) rather
    // than on a NEW independent schedule: NowIndicator's own line therefore
    // stays correct across a local-midnight rollover and re-syncs whenever the
    // user explicitly asks to (Today), but does NOT tick every second/minute
    // on its own — no client-side timer/JS polling loop was added for that
    // (deliberately out of this task's scope: Gantt3's viewport-reconciliation
    // state machine is a carefully-hardened, many-rounds-tested piece of
    // infrastructure — see the campaign ledger's cx9-cx20 entries — and a new
    // autonomous timer-driven StateHasChanged is exactly the kind of "no
    // consumer asked for this render, but state changed anyway" event that
    // machinery's generation/supersession invariants were never exercised
    // against; a live-ticking indicator is a natural follow-up layered on top
    // of this same interop foundation, not a T2 requirement). Guarded on
    // NowIndicator itself so a chart that never enables the feature pays zero
    // extra interop cost.
    //
    // Codex review (phase-3 fix round), P2: those three points alone leave two
    // gaps around a NowIndicator that is off at mount and gets flipped on
    // later (a controlled `NowIndicator` parameter, or any caller re-rendering
    // this component after toggling it): (1) RefreshBrowserNowAsync's own
    // guard above makes the FIRST call (firstRender) a no-op while the option
    // is still false, so _browserNow never gets seeded — GanttTimeline's
    // `Now ?? DateTime.Now` fallback then renders the SERVER's clock instead
    // of the browser's until one of the three existing trigger points happens
    // to run again with the option now true; on a cross-timezone Blazor
    // Server circuit that can be off by hours. (2) toggling off then back on
    // is not itself a trigger either, so a re-enable can just as easily
    // redisplay whatever _browserNow was left holding from hours earlier.
    // OnParametersSetAsync below now treats a false->true transition of the
    // NowIndicator PARAMETER as a fourth trigger, closing both: it fires on
    // the very first pass that turns the option on (mount-with-it-already-on
    // is unaffected — OnAfterRenderAsync's firstRender already covers that
    // case), and it fires again every time a prior disable is followed by a
    // re-enable, so neither gap can reuse a stale value.
    private DateTime? _browserNow;

    // Mirrors the NowIndicator PARAMETER as observed by the last completed
    // OnParametersSetAsync pass (seeded from the mount value in OnInitialized,
    // since the very first pass is a no-op — see its own early return below).
    // Compared against the CURRENT parameter value each subsequent pass purely
    // to detect a false->true edge; this is intentionally a raw edge-detector,
    // not another "controlled vs uncontrolled" discriminator like the
    // Tasks/ViewMode ones above — NowIndicator has no *Changed callback and no
    // uncontrolled-edit state to protect.
    private bool _lastNowIndicator;

    private async Task RefreshBrowserNowAsync()
    {
        if (!NowIndicator) return;
        var iso = await Interop.GanttV3GetLocalDateTimeAsync();
        if (_disposed) return;
        if (iso is null) return; // interop unavailable — GanttTimeline's own DateTime.Now fallback keeps rendering
        if (!DateTime.TryParseExact(iso, "yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, DateTimeStyles.None, out var browserNow)) return;
        _browserNow = browserNow;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        if (!_initialized)
        {
            // The very first parameter pass lands on the mount state OnInitialized
            // already seeded (and GanttTimeline's own firstRender does the initial
            // center) — just adopt it as the reconcile baseline and skip. The
            // mount intent already records this ViewMode as accounted for, so
            // this pass has nothing to claim.
            _initialized = true;
            return;
        }

        // Codex review (phase-3 fix round), P2 — see _lastNowIndicator's own
        // remarks. A false->true edge on the NowIndicator PARAMETER since the
        // last pass is a fourth trigger for _browserNow, alongside mount, the
        // once-per-server-day check, and Today: catches both a chart mounted
        // with the option off that gets turned on later (RefreshBrowserNowAsync's
        // own guard made the firstRender call above a no-op, so _browserNow was
        // never seeded) and a disable-then-re-enable, which would otherwise
        // silently redisplay whatever _browserNow a much earlier enable left
        // behind. Read BEFORE the edge-detector is updated below, and awaited
        // here (not fire-and-forget) so the very same pass that turns the
        // option on already renders the freshly-resolved browser time rather
        // than one paint of the stale/default value followed by a correction.
        var nowIndicatorEnabled = NowIndicator && !_lastNowIndicator;
        _lastNowIndicator = NowIndicator;
        if (nowIndicatorEnabled) await RefreshBrowserNowAsync();

        // Design spec Phase 3, T6 — push a CONTROLLED SelectedIds parameter
        // into _state on every subsequent pass too (see OnInitialized's own
        // remarks for why this needs none of Tasks/ViewMode's hash-diff
        // machinery: GanttState.SetSelectedIds is already a silent no-op when
        // value-equal, so a controlled caller that merely re-supplies its own
        // current selection every render — the common Blazor pattern —
        // doesn't spuriously touch anything). An UNCONTROLLED chart
        // (SelectedIds null) leaves _state.SelectedIds exactly as
        // HandleRowSelectionChangeAsync's own direct writes last left it.
        if (SelectedIds is not null) _state.SetSelectedIds(SelectedIds);

        // Phase 2, T1 fix — see HandleTaskUpdateAsync's own remarks: a
        // drag/resize commit mutates _state DIRECTLY, bypassing the Tasks
        // PARAMETER. Naively handing the raw parameter to ReconcileAsync on
        // every pass would silently revert that edit the next time ANYTHING
        // causes Gantt3 to re-render while an uncontrolled caller's Tasks
        // parameter still holds its stale (pre-drag) value. Resolved exactly
        // like v2's Gantt.razor: UNCONTROLLED (no TasksChanged binding) only
        // applies the parameter when its own value moved since we last
        // looked; CONTROLLED applies it unless it's merely an echo of what
        // HandleTaskUpdateAsync just pushed via TasksChanged (a genuine veto —
        // the parent kept some OTHER value — still wins and rolls the edit
        // back, matching v2). Otherwise the reconciler targets the currently-
        // committed (possibly drag-edited) _state.Tasks, so its own
        // WouldChangeTasks diff sees no change and the edit survives.
        //
        // Merge note (phase-2 x master #382): MaterializeTasks() remains the
        // one legitimate enumeration of the Tasks parameter this pass (its own
        // contract below is unchanged — a replay never re-enumerates), it just
        // no longer flows straight into nextTasks unconditionally.
        //
        // Bug fix (Codex P1 "Preserve uncontrolled edits when GroupBy
        // changes"): parentMoved is meant to answer "did the Tasks PARAMETER
        // itself change" — but hashing the Sorted (GroupBy-applied) list
        // answered a DIFFERENT question: "did the parameter's content OR the
        // current grouping change." SortedTasks reorders by whatever GroupBy
        // is current, so a consumer that changes GroupBy alone (Tasks itself
        // untouched) produced a different hash purely from the reordering —
        // indistinguishable from a genuine parent-initiated Tasks change —
        // which discarded an uncontrolled drag edit that had reconciled to
        // _state.Tasks, exactly the revert this whole mechanism exists to
        // prevent. Hashing the RAW (as-delivered) list instead makes the
        // comparison GroupBy-independent, as it should be: grouping is a
        // display concern, not a signal that the parent's own data moved.
        var (rawTasks, materializedTasks) = MaterializeTasks();
        var incomingHash = ComputeTasksHash(rawTasks);
        var parentMoved = TasksChanged.HasDelegate
            ? incomingHash != _lastPushedTasksHash
            : incomingHash != _lastParentTasksHash;
        _lastParentTasksHash = incomingHash;
        // Whichever task set wins, it must reflect the CURRENT GroupBy — a
        // GroupBy change must still re-sort a preserved uncontrolled edit,
        // just not DISCARD it. materializedTasks (from MaterializeTasks
        // above) is already grouped by construction; SortedTasks on the
        // preserved branch is a no-op re-sort of already-sorted data when
        // GroupBy hasn't actually changed (idempotent, matching structural
        // equality downstream), so this costs nothing extra in the common case.
        var nextTasks = parentMoved ? materializedTasks : SortedTasks(_state.Tasks.ToList());

        // Keep the replay snapshot (_materializedTasks — see its own remarks)
        // in step with what THIS pass actually targets. When the parameter
        // hasn't genuinely moved, that's the possibly drag-edited committed
        // state, not the stale raw parameter MaterializeTasks just captured —
        // otherwise a later replay (a navigation or theme flip superseding an
        // in-flight reconcile, see ReapplyCurrentParametersAsync) would
        // resurrect that stale value via _materializedTasks and silently
        // revert the edit even though this pass itself preserved it.
        if (!parentMoved) _materializedTasks = _state.Tasks.ToList();

        // The one place the ViewMode PARAMETER may author an intent — see
        // ResolveParameterMode. A claim here REPLACES whatever owned the view
        // mode before, including an in-flight toolbar pick (round 16 finding #2's
        // "a competing parameter push wins outright"), so there is no separate
        // "retire the stale toolbar request" step to forget.
        var (incomingMode, claimedIntentId) = ResolveParameterMode();

        // A NON-CLAIMING pass asserts nothing about the mode, so it reconciles to
        // the COMMITTED one — which is what keeps a pass with genuinely nothing
        // to do a total no-op that never claims a generation (round 17 finding
        // #1). But a tasks-only or geometry-only update DOES have work: it claims
        // a generation and commits, superseding whatever reconcile still carries
        // a pending intent, which would then abandon and strand that intent in
        // Applying forever with its mode never applied (round 4's finding).
        //
        // Such a pass therefore CARRIES the pending mode — through its OWN single
        // reconcile, not a replay chained after it (Codex PR-382 review round 7,
        // P1 "Replay against the viewport that is actually rendered"). Round 4
        // chained a second ReconcileAsync here, which committed the first pass's
        // range/snapshot and then immediately captured the live scroll centre for
        // the second — before Blazor had rendered any of it, so the capture read
        // pixels from the OLD DOM and decoded them against the NEW range. One
        // reconcile has no such window: its capture happens before anything
        // commits, exactly as it does for every other trigger, and tasks, mode
        // and geometry then land in one coherent frame (which is also what
        // GanttViewportReconciler already treats as canonical for a simultaneous
        // task+mode change — round 12/14's case 4).
        //
        // Gated on the pass actually having work, asked via the SAME pure
        // decision the reconcile itself uses (PassHasViewportWork): carrying
        // unconditionally would turn round 17's no-op re-render into a
        // generation-claiming pass that aborts the very reconcile about to
        // succeed on its own.
        long? carriedIntentId = null;
        if (claimedIntentId is null
            && _viewModeIntent.IsPending
            && _viewModeIntent.Mode != incomingMode
            && PassHasViewportWork(nextTasks, incomingMode))
        {
            incomingMode = _viewModeIntent.Mode;
            carriedIntentId = _viewModeIntent.Id;
        }

        var outcome = await ReconcileAsync(nextTasks, incomingMode);

        // Superseded: this pass never resolved whether the intent it claimed or
        // carried landed, so that intent stays PENDING for whatever superseded it
        // to replay (round 18-f2). Neither claimed nor carried: this pass authored
        // nothing and must not settle somebody else's in-flight intent.
        var settleIntentId = claimedIntentId ?? carriedIntentId;
        if (outcome == GanttReconcileOutcome.Superseded || settleIntentId is not { } intentId) return;
        await SettleViewModeIntentAsync(intentId, outcome);
    }

    // Whether a reconcile of these inputs would have anything to do at all —
    // asked through the SAME pure decision ReconcileAsync makes, so the two can
    // never disagree about what "nothing changed" means.
    private bool PassHasViewportWork(IReadOnlyList<GanttTask> nextTasks, GanttViewMode mode)
    {
        var tasksChanged = _state.WouldChangeTasks(nextTasks);
        var next = BuildSnapshot(tasksChanged ? _tasksVersion + 1 : _tasksVersion, nextTasks, mode);
        // taskRangeDisjoint only ever ADDS a scroll target, and only when tasks
        // changed — which already forces work — so it cannot affect this answer.
        return !GanttViewportReconciler.Decide(_lastSnapshot, next, taskRangeDisjoint: false).IsNoOp;
    }

    // The last materialized+sorted snapshot of the Tasks PARAMETER (Codex PR-382
    // review round 4, P2 "Reuse the materialized tasks for navigation replays").
    //
    // Tasks is publicly typed as IEnumerable<GanttTask>, so a consumer may hand
    // over a LINQ query, an iterator, or any other single-pass sequence.
    // Enumerating it is only ever legitimate on a PARAMETER pass — the one moment
    // the consumer has actually just handed us the sequence. Internal actions
    // (Previous/Next/Today, a ThemeService direction flip) are not parameter
    // passes: nothing about Tasks can have changed since the last one, so
    // re-enumerating there is both pointless and, for a one-shot sequence,
    // destructive — the second pass yields nothing (or throws) and the reconcile
    // "sees" an empty task set and clears the chart.
    //
    // NOTE: this is NOT a regression from the round-3 ownership consolidation —
    // ReapplyCurrentParametersAsync has re-enumerated Tasks on every navigation
    // since round 18-f2 first introduced it, and ShiftAsync/GoToTodayAsync have
    // routed through it ever since. The consolidation neither added an
    // enumeration site nor changed how often any of them runs; it just made the
    // pre-existing hazard easier to see by putting the replay path under one
    // rule. Fixed here rather than deferred because a single-pass sequence is a
    // perfectly ordinary thing to bind to an IEnumerable parameter.
    private List<GanttTask> _materializedTasks = new();

    // The ONE place the Tasks parameter is enumerated. Called only from the two
    // genuine parameter moments (OnInitialized's mount seed and each
    // OnParametersSetAsync pass); every replay reads _materializedTasks instead.
    //
    // Returns BOTH the raw (as-delivered, un-grouped) list and the sorted
    // (GroupBy-applied) one — see the P1 fix in OnParametersSetAsync's own
    // remarks ("Preserve uncontrolled edits when GroupBy changes") for why a
    // caller needs the raw shape too: hashing the SORTED list for
    // parameter-change detection made a GroupBy change alone look like a
    // genuine Tasks change, since SortedTasks reorders by whatever GroupBy
    // is CURRENT, independent of whether the parameter's own content moved.
    private (List<GanttTask> Raw, List<GanttTask> Sorted) MaterializeTasks()
    {
        var raw = Tasks?.ToList() ?? new List<GanttTask>();
        return (raw, _materializedTasks = SortedTasks(raw));
    }

    private List<GanttTask> SortedTasks(List<GanttTask> raw)
    {
        if (GroupBy is null) return raw;
        return raw
            .Select((t, i) => (t, i))
            .OrderBy(x => GroupBy(x.t))
            .ThenBy(x => x.i)
            .Select(x => x.t)
            .ToList();
    }

    // Faithful port of gantt-v2.js's render() min/max + per-unit padding preamble
    // (gantt-v2.js lines 155-190) — GanttScale.BuildDateUnits deliberately leaves
    // this to its caller (see its own remarks), and this is that caller.
    // Bug fix (Codex round 9 review, P2 #2/#3): two related gaps against v2's
    // own normalizeTasks pipeline (truncate -> filter), both closed here by
    // routing through GanttRowModel's shared normalize+filter step (the SAME
    // one BuildVisibleRows uses — see its own remarks) rather than a second,
    // independently-written copy that could silently diverge:
    //  - the min/max used RAW Start/End (not date-truncated) — a task
    //    carrying a real time-of-day skewed the computed range by that
    //    fractional day, misaligning it against BarGeometry's own (round-8
    //    fixed) date-truncated bar geometry in sub-day modes.
    //  - the min/max read the UNFILTERED task list, so a genuinely invalid
    //    End<Start task (dropped from Rows entirely by GanttRowModel) could
    //    still stretch the computed range even though it renders no row —
    //    the exact skew the round-8 report flagged as out of scope then.
    private static GanttDateRange ComputeInitialRange(GanttViewMode mode, IReadOnlyList<GanttTask> tasks, DateTime today)
    {
        var validTasks = GanttRowModel.FilterValidDurationTasks(tasks);
        DateTime minDate, maxDate;
        if (validTasks.Count > 0)
        {
            minDate = validTasks.Min(t => t.Start.Date);
            maxDate = validTasks.Max(t => (t.IsMilestone ? t.Start : t.End).Date);
        }
        else
        {
            // "today" here is the CALLER's EffectiveToday (Codex round 2, P2 #9
            // — browser-local once resolved, server DateTime.Today until then),
            // not a fresh DateTime.Today read — kept a pure/static/testable
            // method by threading it in as a parameter instead.
            minDate = today.AddDays(-7);
            maxDate = today.AddDays(14);
        }

        return ApplyPadding(mode, minDate, maxDate);
    }

    // Bug fix (Codex round 4, P2 #8): extracted from ComputeInitialRange so
    // HandleViewModeChangedAsync's own recenter (below) can apply the EXACT
    // same per-mode padding math around an arbitrary center date instead of
    // task min/max — a second, independently-written copy of this switch was
    // exactly the kind of divergence risk this codebase avoids elsewhere
    // (see e.g. GanttScale.BarTop's own remarks on the same principle).
    private static GanttDateRange ApplyPadding(GanttViewMode mode, DateTime minDate, DateTime maxDate)
    {
        var cfg = GanttScale.GetConfig(mode);
        return cfg.Unit switch
        {
            GanttScaleUnit.Day => new GanttDateRange(
                minDate.AddDays(-cfg.PadBefore * cfg.Step),
                maxDate.AddDays(cfg.PadAfter * cfg.Step)),
            GanttScaleUnit.Month => new GanttDateRange(
                new DateTime(minDate.Year, minDate.Month, 1, 0, 0, 0, minDate.Kind).AddMonths(-cfg.PadBefore),
                new DateTime(maxDate.Year, maxDate.Month, 1, 0, 0, 0, maxDate.Kind).AddMonths(cfg.PadAfter)),
            GanttScaleUnit.Year => new GanttDateRange(
                new DateTime(minDate.Year - cfg.PadBefore, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, minDate.Kind),
                new DateTime(maxDate.Year + cfg.PadAfter, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, maxDate.Kind)),
            GanttScaleUnit.Hour => new GanttDateRange(
                minDate.AddDays(-Math.Ceiling(cfg.PadBefore * cfg.Step / 24.0)),
                maxDate.AddDays(Math.Ceiling(cfg.PadAfter * cfg.Step / 24.0))),
            // Design spec Phase 3, T2 — Quarter mode. Snaps min/max to their own
            // calendar-quarter start (GanttScale.AlignToUnitStart's Quarter
            // branch — Jan/Apr/Jul/Oct 1), same "unit-align before padding"
            // pattern the Month/Year branches above use, then pads by
            // cfg.PadBefore/PadAfter QUARTERS (*3 months each).
            GanttScaleUnit.Quarter => new GanttDateRange(
                GanttScale.AlignToUnitStart(mode, minDate).AddMonths(-cfg.PadBefore * 3),
                GanttScale.AlignToUnitStart(mode, maxDate).AddMonths(cfg.PadAfter * 3)),
            _ => new GanttDateRange(minDate, maxDate),
        };
    }

    // Design spec Phase 3, T9 — infinite scroll's own "one page" extension
    // amount. Deliberately reuses the SAME cfg.PadBefore/PadAfter magnitude
    // ApplyPadding's own per-unit branches above already apply to a
    // task-derived minDate/maxDate at MOUNT time — "a page" of infinite-scroll
    // growth is therefore exactly as much range as the chart's own initial
    // window already carries as padding, not a new, independently-chosen
    // magnitude. A DELIBERATE near-duplicate of ApplyPadding's own switch
    // rather than a refactor OF it: ApplyPadding's Month/Year/Quarter branches
    // additionally SNAP minDate/maxDate to their own unit boundary first
    // (`new DateTime(minDate.Year, minDate.Month, 1, ...)` etc.) because its
    // own callers pass RAW task dates that are not yet aligned — these two
    // methods are called ONLY with `_state.VisibleRange.Start`/`.End`, which
    // is ALREADY unit-aligned by this codebase's own aligned-origin invariant
    // (every OTHER range-construction path already maintains it), so
    // re-snapping here would be redundant, not just differently-shaped;
    // sharing one method between "snap-then-pad" (ApplyPadding, still used by
    // ComputeInitialRange/the view-mode-switch recenter) and "pad-an-already-
    // aligned-boundary" (these two) would need a bool/branch parameter on a
    // method eight-plus Codex rounds already hardened, for a net decrease in
    // clarity — a considered trade-off, not an oversight (flagged for the
    // reviewer).
    private static DateTime PadBoundaryBefore(GanttViewMode mode, DateTime date)
    {
        var cfg = GanttScale.GetConfig(mode);
        return cfg.Unit switch
        {
            GanttScaleUnit.Day => date.AddDays(-cfg.PadBefore * cfg.Step),
            GanttScaleUnit.Month => date.AddMonths(-cfg.PadBefore),
            GanttScaleUnit.Year => date.AddYears(-cfg.PadBefore),
            GanttScaleUnit.Hour => date.AddDays(-Math.Ceiling(cfg.PadBefore * cfg.Step / 24.0)),
            GanttScaleUnit.Quarter => date.AddMonths(-cfg.PadBefore * 3),
            _ => date,
        };
    }

    // See PadBoundaryBefore's own remarks — the mirror-image TRAILING (later)
    // extension, using cfg.PadAfter instead of cfg.PadBefore.
    private static DateTime PadBoundaryAfter(GanttViewMode mode, DateTime date)
    {
        var cfg = GanttScale.GetConfig(mode);
        return cfg.Unit switch
        {
            GanttScaleUnit.Day => date.AddDays(cfg.PadAfter * cfg.Step),
            GanttScaleUnit.Month => date.AddMonths(cfg.PadAfter),
            GanttScaleUnit.Year => date.AddYears(cfg.PadAfter),
            GanttScaleUnit.Hour => date.AddDays(Math.Ceiling(cfg.PadAfter * cfg.Step / 24.0)),
            GanttScaleUnit.Quarter => date.AddMonths(cfg.PadAfter * 3),
            _ => date,
        };
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Handles <see cref="GanttTimeline.OnRangeExtensionRequested"/> (design
    /// spec Phase 3, T9) — <c>GanttTimeline</c> only ever ASKS (see its own
    /// remarks); this is the ONE place that actually extends
    /// <c>_state.VisibleRange</c>, deliberately OUTSIDE <see cref="ReconcileAsync"/>'s
    /// own pipeline (same reasoning as <see cref="HandleTaskUpdateAsync"/>'s
    /// own direct-commit range-expansion path immediately below in this file:
    /// routing a purely local, DOM-scroll-driven range growth through the
    /// tasks/mode/geometry reconciler would be the wrong tool — nothing about
    /// tasks, view mode, or layout geometry changed here).
    ///
    /// <c>leading</c> (earlier dates, <c>VisibleRange.Start</c> moves earlier):
    /// the coordinate ORIGIN shifts (see <c>GanttScale.BuildDateUnits</c>' own
    /// "origin = units[0] = the first date unit" contract) — every bar/arrow/
    /// header X value everywhere in the chart rebases by the SAME pixel delta
    /// the instant this commits, so the live scroll position must be corrected
    /// in the SAME coherent frame or the user sees a jump. Reuses the
    /// EXISTING <see cref="ResolveCurrentCenterDateAsync"/>/<see cref="EmitScrollIntent"/>
    /// pipeline UNCHANGED — the identical "capture the live center as a DATE
    /// under the OLD geometry, recompute its pixel under the NEW geometry,
    /// recenter" dance <c>ReconcileAsync</c>'s own <c>SelfCenteredOnCapture</c>
    /// branch already performs for a view-mode switch (a DIFFERENT kind of
    /// origin shift, same underlying hazard) — NOT a second, bespoke
    /// "translate scrollLeft by a computed pixel delta" implementation: this
    /// codebase's constraint is "no second scroll channel, no second origin
    /// implementation," and centering on the SAME captured date under the NEW
    /// origin is PROVABLY pixel-exact here (not merely approximate), because
    /// <c>GanttScale.DateToPixel</c> is linear/additive in its own
    /// <c>origin</c> parameter for EVERY unit branch, GIVEN both origins are
    /// unit-aligned (this codebase's own standing invariant — see
    /// <c>GanttScale.AlignToUnitStart</c>'s remarks): the Month branch's own
    /// day-fraction term depends only on the TARGET date's day-of-month, never
    /// on the origin's (always day-1), so a changed origin shifts every
    /// pixel by EXACTLY the same constant, with no re-derivation error beyond
    /// ordinary double-precision arithmetic — recentering on the identical
    /// date reproduces the identical viewport, to within CSS/layout rounding
    /// (the E2E ±1px tolerance measures THAT rounding, not an algorithmic
    /// approximation).
    ///
    /// <c>trailing</c> (later dates, <c>VisibleRange.End</c> grows): the
    /// origin is UNCHANGED (<c>VisibleRange.Start</c> doesn't move) — every
    /// EXISTING bar/arrow/header X value is therefore unaffected by
    /// construction, so this branch skips the capture/recenter dance
    /// entirely (cheaper, and correctly a no-op on scroll position).
    ///
    /// Gesture-suppression (decision 3): <see cref="GanttTimeline"/>'s own
    /// gate (<c>TryRequestRangeExtensionAsync</c>) already checked
    /// <c>GanttV3HasActiveDragAsync</c> once before ever calling here — this
    /// method re-checks it AGAIN, immediately before committing, but ONLY on
    /// the <c>leading</c> path: that is the ONLY path with an await
    /// (<see cref="ResolveCurrentCenterDateAsync"/>'s own live-scroll-center
    /// read) between GanttTimeline's own check and the actual commit, i.e.
    /// the only path where a gesture could start DURING this method's own
    /// window. The <c>trailing</c> path has no such await — nothing can
    /// interleave between GanttTimeline's already-fresh check and this
    /// synchronous commit, so a second check here would test nothing new.
    /// Abandoning (not rebasing, unlike <see cref="HandleTaskUpdateAsync"/>'s
    /// own concurrent-edit handling) is deliberate: this is a level-triggered,
    /// retryable request (the very next qualifying scroll report re-asks), not
    /// a user EDIT that must never be silently dropped.
    ///
    /// Decision 3's own full gesture-channel enumeration (every pointer
    /// session gantt-v3.js currently registers, not just the two that
    /// suppress): (1) bar move/resize/progress drag
    /// (<c>activeBarDrags</c>/<c>registerDrag</c>) — SUPPRESSES, because the
    /// drag's own ghost element is positioned via the SAME origin-relative
    /// pixel math (<c>GanttScale.DateToPixel</c>) an extension rebases (the
    /// Phase-2 ghost-math lesson this task's own plan calls out by name).
    /// (2) drag-create on an empty track (<c>activeCreateDrags</c>/
    /// <c>startCreateDrag</c>) — SUPPRESSES, for the identical reason: its
    /// own preview rect is origin-relative too. (3) the tree/timeline
    /// splitter (<c>registerSplitterDrag</c>, design spec Phase 3, T5) —
    /// does NOT suppress: its live-drag visual is a single CSS custom
    /// property (<c>--lumeo-gantt-tree-name-width</c>) that only ever
    /// affects the tree pane's own column width, never a date-derived X/Y —
    /// an origin shift mid-splitter-drag has literally nothing for that
    /// gesture to desync against. (4) tree-row drag-reorder
    /// (<c>registerRowReorderDrag</c>, design spec Phase 3, T6) — does NOT
    /// suppress: its ghost row and drop-index line are VERTICAL-position-only
    /// (sibling list index, <c>data-reorder-index</c>), never a function of
    /// the horizontal date origin either. (5) the bar right-click context
    /// menu (<c>registerBarContextMenu</c>, design spec Phase 3, T8) — does
    /// NOT suppress: it is a one-shot popover positioned once at the pointer
    /// coordinates the OS-level contextmenu event reports, not a continuous
    /// gesture that tracks origin-relative geometry over time (an origin
    /// shift after it opens cannot move an already-positioned popover). Only
    /// (1) and (2) touch <c>activeDragGestureCount</c> — see its own JS
    /// declaration for the identical reasoning from that side.
    ///
    /// Decision 4 (unbounded growth): this method's own two range-growth
    /// branches each additionally cap against
    /// <see cref="GanttScale.MaxInfiniteScrollRangeUnits"/> before committing
    /// — see that constant's own remarks for the full "hard cap vs. far-side
    /// trimming vs. documented-unbounded, what breaks first" decision.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task HandleRangeExtensionRequestAsync(bool leading)
    {
        if (_disposed || !InfiniteScroll) return;

        if (leading)
        {
            var modeBeforeCapture = _state.ViewMode;
            var rangeBeforeCapture = _state.VisibleRange;
            // Reads the LIVE logical scroll center as a raw PIXEL (the same
            // underlying interop call ResolveCurrentCenterDateAsync itself
            // wraps — see ScrollTargetXOverride's own remarks in
            // GanttTimeline.razor for why this leading-edge recenter reads it
            // directly instead, bypassing that method's own DateTime
            // conversion entirely to keep sub-column precision that a
            // captured DATE would lose on its way back through
            // GanttScale.DateToPixel).
            var leadingOffset = ScrollHostLeadingOffset;
            var directionArg = EffectiveDirection == LayoutDirection.Rtl ? "rtl" : "ltr";
            var logicalCenterX = await Interop.GanttV3GetScrollCenterXAsync(_scrollPaneRef, directionArg);
            if (_disposed) return;

            // Something else (a mode switch, a geometry reconcile) committed
            // while the capture above was in flight — the captured center was
            // decoded under geometry that is no longer current. Abandon
            // rather than risk applying a stale decode; the next qualifying
            // scroll report re-requests if the user is still near the edge.
            if (_state.ViewMode != modeBeforeCapture || _state.VisibleRange != rangeBeforeCapture) return;

            // Decision 3's own re-check — see this method's own remarks for
            // why only THIS path needs it.
            if (await Interop.GanttV3HasActiveDragAsync()) return;
            if (_disposed || _state.ViewMode != modeBeforeCapture || _state.VisibleRange != rangeBeforeCapture) return;

            var newStart = PadBoundaryBefore(_state.ViewMode, _state.VisibleRange.Start);
            if (newStart >= _state.VisibleRange.Start) return; // defensive no-op guard — should be unreachable (every mode's Pad constant is >= 0)

            // Decision 4 (unbounded growth) — see GanttScale.MaxInfiniteScrollRangeUnits'
            // own remarks for the full "hard cap, what breaks first" reasoning.
            // A no-op here is the SAME "this edge simply stops extending" shape
            // as InfiniteScroll=false's own v2-parity dead-end — nothing to
            // recenter, nothing to commit, the next qualifying scroll report
            // re-asks and gets the same answer (level-triggered, like every
            // other no-op branch in this method).
            if (GanttScale.BuildDateUnits(_state.ViewMode, newStart, _state.VisibleRange.End).Count > GanttScale.MaxInfiniteScrollRangeUnits) return;

            // The EXACT pixel distance the origin just moved by — pure
            // GanttScale.DateToPixel origin-linearity (see
            // ScrollTargetXOverride's own remarks), no DateTime round trip.
            var columnWidth = EffectiveColumnWidthFor(_state.ViewMode);
            var addedPixels = GanttScale.DateToPixel(_state.ViewMode, newStart, _state.VisibleRange.Start, columnWidth);
            // Captured BEFORE SetVisibleRange below — VisibleCenterDate is a
            // LIVE computed property reading _state.VisibleRange directly, so
            // reading it AFTER the mutation would silently return the NEW
            // (post-extension) range's own midpoint instead of the fallback
            // this branch actually needs: the OLD range's center, the exact
            // same value ResolveCurrentCenterDateAsync's own null fallback
            // would have used.
            var fallbackCenter = VisibleCenterDate;

            _state.SetVisibleRange(newStart, _state.VisibleRange.End);

            if (logicalCenterX is { } x)
            {
                // Pure pixel translation: the SAME logical center, shifted by
                // EXACTLY the origin's own move — see
                // GanttTimeline.ScrollTargetXOverride's own remarks for the
                // full derivation (this is provably a same-frame, ±0px
                // correction modulo ordinary CSS/layout rounding, not merely
                // "close").
                EmitScrollIntent(null, pixelOverride: (x - leadingOffset) + addedPixels);
            }
            else
            {
                // Pane not measurable (JS interop unavailable, or not yet
                // laid out) — the SAME range-midpoint fallback
                // ResolveCurrentCenterDateAsync itself would have used.
                EmitScrollIntent(fallbackCenter);
            }
        }
        else
        {
            var newEnd = PadBoundaryAfter(_state.ViewMode, _state.VisibleRange.End);
            if (newEnd <= _state.VisibleRange.End) return; // defensive no-op guard — see the leading branch's own remarks
            // Decision 4 — see the leading branch's own remarks + GanttScale.MaxInfiniteScrollRangeUnits.
            if (GanttScale.BuildDateUnits(_state.ViewMode, _state.VisibleRange.Start, newEnd).Count > GanttScale.MaxInfiniteScrollRangeUnits) return;
            _state.SetVisibleRange(_state.VisibleRange.Start, newEnd);
            // No scroll intent — see this method's own class remarks: a
            // trailing extension never shifts the origin, so the DOM's
            // current scroll position remains exactly correct.
        }
    }

    // Codex round 10 review, P2 #4 — see GoToTodayAsync's own remarks. The
    // number of Month-unit columns GanttScale.BuildDateUnits would generate
    // between two ALREADY unit-aligned dates (both are day-1 of their own
    // month, per this codebase's aligned-origin invariant), independent of
    // day-of-month.
    private static int MonthsBetween(DateTime start, DateTime end) =>
        (end.Year - start.Year) * 12 + (end.Month - start.Month);

    private string PeriodLabel
    {
        get
        {
            var range = _state.VisibleRange;
            return _state.ViewMode switch
            {
                GanttViewMode.Year => $"{range.Start.Year}–{range.End.Year}",
                GanttViewMode.Month => range.Start.ToString("MMMM yyyy", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture),
                // Design spec Phase 3, T2 — Quarter mode. No v2 counterpart to
                // match; "Q{n} {year} – Q{n} {year}" mirrors the Year branch's
                // own "{start}–{end}" range shape at the coarser Quarter grain.
                GanttViewMode.Quarter =>
                    $"Q{(range.Start.Month - 1) / 3 + 1} {range.Start.Year} – Q{(range.End.Month - 1) / 3 + 1} {range.End.Year}",
                _ => $"{range.Start.ToString("MMM d, yyyy", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)} – {range.End.ToString("MMM d, yyyy", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)}",
            };
        }
    }

    // The GanttNav toolbar's zoom trigger — a thin wrapper over
    // ApplyViewModeIntentAsync tagging the claim as GanttViewModeSource.Toolbar.
    private Task HandleViewModeChangedAsync(GanttViewMode mode) =>
        ApplyViewModeIntentAsync(mode, GanttViewModeSource.Toolbar);

    // A mode-only change against the CURRENT task set — routed through the SAME
    // reconcile every other trigger uses (Codex round 14), which decides it as a
    // self-centered recenter that preserves the live scroll position (capture
    // live center, reshape the range around it, emit a one-shot scroll intent).
    // No separate ApplyViewModeChangeAsync/RecenterOn copy of that pipeline
    // anymore.
    //
    // Design spec Phase 3, T4 — SHARED by two callers now, distinguished only by
    // SOURCE: HandleViewModeChangedAsync (the toolbar, above) and
    // IGanttStateHost.SetViewModeAsync (an imperative GanttState.SetViewMode
    // call, routed here by the attach contract — see that method's own remarks).
    // This is deliberately the SAME pipeline, not a parallel one: an imperative
    // caller gets every invariant PR #382 already hardened here (the generation
    // guard, Notifying-phase pending-rejection handling, navigation replay, the
    // disposal guard inside ReconcileAsync) for free, because it is not a new
    // mutation path — it is this exact method, entered from a third call site.
    // The only place `source` is actually consulted downstream is
    // SettleViewModeIntentAsync's owesConsumerEcho check, which now treats
    // Imperative exactly like Toolbar (a controlled consumer is asked to ratify
    // an imperative pick the same way it would a user's own toolbar click).
    private async Task ApplyViewModeIntentAsync(GanttViewMode mode, GanttViewModeSource source)
    {
        if (mode == _state.ViewMode) return;

        // Claim the intent BEFORE the reconcile can yield into its live-center
        // capture, so anything that supersedes that reconcile replays THIS mode
        // rather than losing it (round 19 finding #1) — and so the current
        // ViewMode parameter value is recorded as accounted-for by the same
        // write, since this pick deliberately overrides it (round 20 finding B).
        var intent = ClaimViewModeIntent(mode, source);

        var outcome = await ReconcileAsync(_state.Tasks, mode);
        // Superseded/no-op: suppress every side effect, not just the commit
        // (round 16 finding #2) — including the consumer echo, which would
        // otherwise report a mode that was never applied. The intent is
        // deliberately left alone: whatever superseded this call either replays
        // it or has already replaced it with its own.
        if (outcome != GanttReconcileOutcome.Committed) return;

        // Settling this intent is what performs the controlled echo (and only
        // then advances the parameter accounting) — see SettleViewModeIntentAsync.
        await SettleViewModeIntentAsync(intent.Id, outcome);
    }

    // Bug fix (Codex round 5, P2 #5): reads the shared scroll pane's ACTUAL
    // current logical center via JS (see GanttV3GetScrollCenterXAsync's own
    // remarks), subtracts the SAME ScrollHostLeadingOffset GanttTimeline adds
    // when scrolling TO a target (so this is a genuine, direction-correct
    // inverse of that write path — the offset doesn't change with ViewMode,
    // only with EffectiveShowTreePane/EffectiveDirection, neither of which a
    // mode switch touches), then converts that Timeline-relative pixel back
    // into a date against the OUTGOING (still current at this point — called
    // before SetViewMode below) mode's own Origin/column width, replicating
    // exactly what GanttTimeline itself would compute as its own Origin for
    // the SAME RangeStart/RangeEnd/ViewMode inputs it's currently rendering
    // against.
    //
    // Bug fix (Codex round 6, P1 #1): this used to go through
    // GanttScale.PixelToDate — the DRAG-snapping inverse, which rounds to the
    // nearest WHOLE month/year in those view modes. That rounding is correct
    // for drag math but far too coarse here: Month/Year's unit is huge
    // relative to a typical scroll-pixel delta, so the preserved center
    // silently snapped to whichever month/year boundary was nearest,
    // visibly jumping on repeated switches instead of tracking the actual
    // scrolled-to position. GanttScale.PixelToDateContinuous is the
    // non-snapping counterpart added specifically for this call site — see
    // its own remarks; PixelToDate itself is untouched and still backs every
    // drag/resize computation.
